WEBVTT

1
00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:42.095
A philosopher once asked,

2
00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:46.700
"Are we human
because we gaze at the stars,

3
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.477
"or do we gaze at them
because we are human?"

4
00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:52.976
Pointless, really.

5
00:00:53.774 --> 00:00:55.947
Do the stars gaze back?

6
00:00:56.943 --> 00:00:58.820
Now, that's a question.

7
00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:02.682
But I'm getting ahead of myself.

8
00:01:02.682 --> 00:01:05.952
Our story really begins here,

9
00:01:05.952 --> 00:01:09.689
150 years ago at the 
Royal Academy of Science ...

10
00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:12.032
in London, England,

11
00:01:12.926 --> 00:01:17.875
where a letter arrived,
containing a very strange inquiry.

12
00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:22.035
It had come from a country boy ...

13
00:01:22.035 --> 00:01:27.307
and the scientist who read it thought it
might be a practical joke of some kind.

14
00:01:27.307 --> 00:01:30.077
But he duly wrote a reply ...

15
00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:33.285
politely explaining
that the query was nonsense.

16
00:01:34.114 --> 00:01:39.419
And posted it to the boy who 
lived in a village called Wall,

17
00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:43.623
so named, the boy had said,
for the wall that ran alongside it.

18
00:01:43.623 --> 00:01:49.696
A wall that, according to local folklore,
hid an extraordinary secret.

19
00:01:49.696 --> 00:01:54.267
I'm charged with guarding the 
portal to another world.

20
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:58.805
And you're asking me
to just let you through?

21
00:01:58.805 --> 00:02:02.809
Yes. Because, let's be honest, 
it's a field.

22
00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:06.279
Look, do you see
another world out there?

23
00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:10.984
No. You see a field.
Do you see anything non-human?

24
00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:14.588
No. And you know why?
Because it's a field!

25
00:02:14.588 --> 00:02:17.924
Hundreds of years,
this wall's been here.

26
00:02:17.924 --> 00:02:22.796
Hundreds of years, this gap's 
been under 24-hour guard.

27
00:02:22.796 --> 00:02:25.732
- Well ...
- One more word,

28
00:02:25.732 --> 00:02:29.736
and I'll have you up
in front of the village council!

29
00:02:31.238 --> 00:02:34.548
- Well, that sounds rather final.
- Yeah.

30
00:02:35.942 --> 00:02:40.982
- Better just go home, then, I suppose.
- Right, then. Night, Dunstan.

31
00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:43.954
Give my best to your father.

32
00:02:46.086 --> 00:02:47.257
Stop!

33
00:02:49.656 --> 00:02:50.793
Stop!

34
00:03:35.302 --> 00:03:38.004
I don't deal with time-wasters.

35
00:03:38.004 --> 00:03:39.906
Get over here and tend this stall.

36
00:03:39.906 --> 00:03:42.886
I'm off to The Slaughtered Prince
for a pint.

37
00:03:49.182 --> 00:03:51.059
See anything you like?

38
00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.589
Definitely.

39
00:03:55.589 --> 00:04:00.299
I mean, what I meant was 
these ones, the blue ones.

40
00:04:01.161 --> 00:04:02.929
How much are they?

41
00:04:02.929 --> 00:04:05.899
They might be the 
color of your hair.

42
00:04:05.899 --> 00:04:10.136
Or they might be all of your 
memories before you were three.

43
00:04:10.136 --> 00:04:12.505
I can check if you like.

44
00:04:12.505 --> 00:04:16.976
Anyway, you shouldn't buy the 
bluebells. Buy this one instead.

45
00:04:17.844 --> 00:04:19.254
Snowdrop.

46
00:04:21.214 --> 00:04:23.125
It'll bring you luck.

47
00:04:24.451 --> 00:04:26.430
But what does that cost?

48
00:04:27.153 --> 00:04:30.065
This one costs a kiss.

49
00:04:50.176 --> 00:04:51.677
Is she gone?

50
00:04:53.647 --> 00:04:54.989
Follow me.

51
00:05:08.395 --> 00:05:11.102
I'm a princess, tricked into 
being a witch's slave.

52
00:05:11.498 --> 00:05:13.068
Will you liberate me?

53
00:05:22.242 --> 00:05:25.814
It's an enchanted chain.
I'll only be free when she dies.

54
00:05:28.181 --> 00:05:29.316
Sorry.

55
00:05:29.316 --> 00:05:33.059
Well, if I can't liberate you,
what do you want of me?

56
00:05:51.371 --> 00:05:53.407
So, the scientist was wrong.

57
00:05:54.774 --> 00:05:57.444
The wall had successfully 
done its job ...

58
00:05:57.444 --> 00:06:01.815
of hiding the magical 
kingdom of Stormhold.

59
00:06:01.815 --> 00:06:05.785
The young man returned that night
to his home in England,

60
00:06:05.785 --> 00:06:09.122
hoping that his adventure
would soon be forgotten.

61
00:06:09.122 --> 00:06:14.128
But nine months later, he 
received an unexpected souvenir.

62
00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:27.777
This was left at the wall for you.
It says here his name is Tristan.

63
00:06:52.332 --> 00:06:54.834
Tristan? Don't forget the flowers.

64
00:06:54.834 --> 00:06:57.473
Yes. I've got them. 
Thank you, Father.

65
00:06:58.838 --> 00:07:03.343
Eighteen years passed,
and the baby Tristan grew up ...

66
00:07:03.343 --> 00:07:06.722
knowing nothing
of his unconventional heritage.

67
00:07:08.181 --> 00:07:12.519
But never mind
how the infant became a boy.

68
00:07:12.519 --> 00:07:16.865
This is the story of
how Tristan Thorn becomes a man,

69
00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.227
a much greater challenge altogether.

70
00:07:21.194 --> 00:07:23.596
For to achieve it,

71
00:07:23.596 --> 00:07:27.703
he must win the heart
of his one true love.

72
00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.712
- It's him!
- It's him!

73
00:07:35.875 --> 00:07:37.149
Humphrey?

74
00:07:38.211 --> 00:07:40.748
- No, it's Tristan.
- Oh.

75
00:07:42.182 --> 00:07:44.350
Did I leave something at the shop?

76
00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.830
No. I just thought 
I could bring ...

77
00:07:48.121 --> 00:07:50.356
Tristan Thorn,

78
00:07:50.356 --> 00:07:53.359
shop boy by day, 
peeping Tom by night.

79
00:07:53.359 --> 00:07:55.668
Is there no end to your charms?

80
00:07:56.629 --> 00:07:58.865
Humphrey,
there's no need to be like that.

81
00:07:58.865 --> 00:08:00.844
Be nice to the poor boy.

82
00:08:02.101 --> 00:08:04.080
Were those for Victoria?

83
00:08:18.284 --> 00:08:21.521
You were always useless at fencing
in school, Tristan.

84
00:08:21.521 --> 00:08:24.958
In fact, I'm having trouble remembering
if there was anything you were good at.

85
00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:27.404
Humphrey, that's enough.

86
00:08:32.031 --> 00:08:34.200
Are you all right?

87
00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.474
Yeah. Yeah, fine.

88
00:08:58.558 --> 00:09:00.527
- Want some breakfast?
- No, I'm really late for work.

89
00:09:00.527 --> 00:09:04.364
- Are you all right?
- Yeah. Fine. Why?

90
00:09:04.364 --> 00:09:08.968
Oh, I don't know.
Last night, how'd it go?

91
00:09:08.968 --> 00:09:12.415
Oh, really good. 
Really, really good.

92
00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:29.022
Hello, Tristan.

93
00:09:29.022 --> 00:09:31.559
- Victoria.
- Pound of sugar, please.

94
00:09:33.626 --> 00:09:37.764
- Yes.
- Let's see, a bag of flour, a dozen eggs.

95
00:09:37.764 --> 00:09:40.934
Oh, look, I'm sorry about last 
night. Humphrey was really rude.

96
00:09:40.934 --> 00:09:44.574
I also need a sack of potatoes
and some chocolate, please.

97
00:09:48.308 --> 00:09:50.811
May I perhaps see you tonight?

98
00:09:52.245 --> 00:09:54.349
No, but you may walk me home.

99
00:09:55.615 --> 00:09:56.752
Now?

100
00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.422
Yes. Yes, I can.

101
00:10:06.726 --> 00:10:08.603
Father, I lost my job.

102
00:10:10.129 --> 00:10:13.032
Father, I don't ... 
I lost my job. I'm sorry.

103
00:10:13.032 --> 00:10:15.273
- Father ...
- You lost your job.

104
00:10:16.035 --> 00:10:17.639
Yes, so I heard.

105
00:10:18.838 --> 00:10:20.783
Father, I'm sorry. I ...

106
00:10:21.774 --> 00:10:24.777
Maybe Mr. Monday was right.
Maybe I am deluding myself.

107
00:10:24.777 --> 00:10:29.115
- I'm not good enough for Victoria.
- He said that? That's poppycock.

108
00:10:29.115 --> 00:10:30.984
You really want to know
how it went last night?

109
00:10:30.984 --> 00:10:32.121
Yeah.

110
00:10:32.986 --> 00:10:34.294
Not good.

111
00:10:35.221 --> 00:10:37.792
Come on. I'm wasting my time.

112
00:10:38.458 --> 00:10:40.335
I'm not like Humphrey.

113
00:10:40.827 --> 00:10:45.799
Tristan, I can tell you that every man
I ever envied when I was a boy ...

114
00:10:46.499 --> 00:10:48.706
has led an unremarkable life.

115
00:10:49.802 --> 00:10:54.444
So you don't fit with the popular crowd.
Now, I take that as a very good omen.

116
00:11:07.453 --> 00:11:10.289
- Tristan, I clearly said ...
- I know. You told me not to come.

117
00:11:10.289 --> 00:11:12.928
I have something for you. 
A surprise.

118
00:11:13.993 --> 00:11:15.130
No ...

119
00:11:29.375 --> 00:11:32.515
It's not my birthday for 
another week, you know.

120
00:11:36.049 --> 00:11:39.428
- I've never had champagne before.
- Yeah, me, neither.

121
00:11:40.787 --> 00:11:43.199
My God! This is delicious!

122
00:11:44.924 --> 00:11:47.927
How did ... Well, how does a 
shop boy afford all of this?

123
00:11:47.927 --> 00:11:49.667
I'm not a shop boy.

124
00:11:50.563 --> 00:11:52.865
God! I heard. I'm sorry.
What are you going to do now?

125
00:11:52.865 --> 00:11:56.736
No. I mean, I'm not a shop boy.
I was just working in a shop.

126
00:11:56.736 --> 00:12:00.073
And now I'm not. Now I'm free 
to live my life as I wish.

127
00:12:00.073 --> 00:12:02.642
This must have been 
all your savings.

128
00:12:02.642 --> 00:12:05.778
So? I can make more.
That's the beauty of it.

129
00:12:05.778 --> 00:12:07.480
I never intended to stay in Wall,
Victoria.

130
00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.917
There's a big world out there,
I'm gonna make my fortune.

131
00:12:10.917 --> 00:12:13.653
Now you sound just like Humphrey.
He's quite a traveler.

132
00:12:13.653 --> 00:12:17.990
Do you know he's going all the way
to Ipswich just to buy me a ring?

133
00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:19.525
Ipswich?

134
00:12:19.525 --> 00:12:23.596
Victoria, I'm talking about London
or Paris or ...

135
00:12:23.596 --> 00:12:27.500
A ring? Why is he ...
What kind of a ring?

136
00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:30.503
The word is he's planning to 
propose to me on my birthday.

137
00:12:30.503 --> 00:12:32.107
He's going to ...

138
00:12:33.406 --> 00:12:34.907
And you're gonna say yes.

139
00:12:34.907 --> 00:12:38.544
I can't exactly say no after 
he's gone all the way to Ipswich.

140
00:12:38.544 --> 00:12:40.747
"All the way to Ipswich"?

141
00:12:40.747 --> 00:12:45.696
Victoria, for your hand in marriage,
I'd cross oceans or continents.

142
00:12:46.486 --> 00:12:48.087
- Really?
- Yes.

143
00:12:48.087 --> 00:12:49.589
Victoria, for your hand in marriage,

144
00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:53.059
I would go to the gold 
fields of San Francisco ...

145
00:12:53.059 --> 00:12:55.562
and bring you back 
your weight in gold.

146
00:12:56.429 --> 00:12:57.703
I would.

147
00:12:58.598 --> 00:13:02.671
I'd go to Africa and bring you back
a diamond as big as your fist.

148
00:13:03.369 --> 00:13:06.339
Or I'd go to the Arctic
and I'd slaughter a polar bear ...

149
00:13:06.339 --> 00:13:08.512
and bring you back its head.

150
00:13:09.809 --> 00:13:11.583
A polar bear's head?

151
00:13:16.249 --> 00:13:18.126
You're funny, Tristan.

152
00:13:19.218 --> 00:13:23.166
People like you and people 
like me, we're just not ...

153
00:13:26.993 --> 00:13:28.561
I should be going. 
It's really late.

154
00:13:28.561 --> 00:13:32.133
Well, hold on, then. Let's at 
least finish the champagne.

155
00:13:33.299 --> 00:13:34.436
Okay.

156
00:13:38.838 --> 00:13:42.942
Had Tristan known then
how the stars watched Earth,

157
00:13:42.942 --> 00:13:44.844
he'd have shuddered 
at the very thought ...

158
00:13:44.844 --> 00:13:47.290
of an audience to his humiliation.

159
00:13:49.715 --> 00:13:51.517
But, fortunately for him,

160
00:13:51.517 --> 00:13:54.587
nearly every star in the sky
was at that moment ...

161
00:13:54.587 --> 00:13:58.825
looking in earnest at the land
on the other side of the wall,

162
00:13:58.825 --> 00:14:02.495
where the King of all Stormhold
lay on his deathbed,

163
00:14:02.495 --> 00:14:06.499
which was a coincidence because 
it was the King's final act ...

164
00:14:06.499 --> 00:14:11.914
that would change the course
of Tristan's destiny forever.

165
00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.085
Where is Secundus?

166
00:14:16.976 --> 00:14:19.145
He's on his way, Father.

167
00:14:19.145 --> 00:14:20.885
Then we shall wait.

168
00:14:30.323 --> 00:14:34.532
Sorry I'm late, Father.
I came as swiftly as I was able.

169
00:14:39.398 --> 00:14:40.672
Septimus.

170
00:14:41.067 --> 00:14:42.307
Primus.

171
00:14:43.402 --> 00:14:44.642
Tertius.

172
00:14:45.371 --> 00:14:49.785
So, to the matter of succession.

173
00:14:51.744 --> 00:14:56.749
Of my seven sons, there are 
four of you today still standing.

174
00:14:56.749 --> 00:15:01.153
This is quite a break with 
tradition. I had 12 brothers.

175
00:15:01.153 --> 00:15:03.055
And you killed them 
all for your throne ...

176
00:15:03.055 --> 00:15:06.126
before your father, the King,
even felt poorly.

177
00:15:06.826 --> 00:15:09.762
We know, Father.
You're strong and courageous.

178
00:15:09.762 --> 00:15:13.675
And cunning. 
Most importantly, cunning.

179
00:15:14.934 --> 00:15:18.204
- Secundus.
- Yes, Father?

180
00:15:18.204 --> 00:15:22.550
Look through the window.
Tell me what you see.

181
00:15:29.348 --> 00:15:31.418
I see the kingdom, Father.

182
00:15:33.119 --> 00:15:34.921
The whole of Stormhold.

183
00:15:34.921 --> 00:15:38.724
- And?
- My kingdom?

184
00:15:38.724 --> 00:15:41.170
Maybe. Look up.

185
00:16:07.153 --> 00:16:12.796
- Secundus.
- Sextus. Quartus. Quintus.

186
00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:18.164
You're alive. You're ...

187
00:16:18.164 --> 00:16:21.110
Stuck like this till 
the new king is crowned.

188
00:16:21.834 --> 00:16:24.136
I was that close.

189
00:16:24.136 --> 00:16:26.505
Well, at least
you haven't lost your looks.

190
00:16:26.505 --> 00:16:29.508
Oh, please. You're not annoyed
about that whole murder thing, are you?

191
00:16:29.508 --> 00:16:32.611
- I mean, that was 10 years ago.
- Yeah.

192
00:16:32.611 --> 00:16:34.613
Great deal of good it did you, 
didn't it, killing me, Secundus?

193
00:16:34.613 --> 00:16:37.984
Because now, of course,
now you are King of all Stormhold.

194
00:16:37.984 --> 00:16:40.953
Oh, sorry. Wait. No, you're not.
You're dead.

195
00:16:40.953 --> 00:16:44.223
Una? Una?

196
00:16:44.223 --> 00:16:48.330
No, Father, it's me, your son.

197
00:16:49.195 --> 00:16:50.469
Tertius.

198
00:16:52.832 --> 00:16:55.668
Where is your sister, Una?

199
00:16:55.668 --> 00:16:59.071
Sorry, Father. Nobody has 
seen Una for years now.

200
00:16:59.071 --> 00:17:01.374
- Septimus?
- What?

201
00:17:01.374 --> 00:17:06.979
Tradition dictates the throne 
must pass to a male heir.

202
00:17:06.979 --> 00:17:09.215
Exactly, Father.

203
00:17:09.215 --> 00:17:12.051
So why would I kill my sister
when these cretins are still alive?

204
00:17:12.051 --> 00:17:13.291
Indeed.

205
00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:20.636
Therefore, we shall resolve the situation
in a non-traditional manner.

206
00:17:44.283 --> 00:17:49.755
Only he of royal blood
can restore the ruby.

207
00:17:49.755 --> 00:17:56.433
And the one of you that does so
shall be the new King of Stormhold.

208
00:18:47.012 --> 00:18:49.719
Oh, Tristan! A shooting star!

209
00:18:50.983 --> 00:18:52.325
Beautiful.

210
00:18:55.488 --> 00:18:58.491
More beautiful than a fancy ring
from Ipswich?

211
00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:06.165
Victoria, for your hand in marriage,

212
00:19:06.165 --> 00:19:09.874
I'd cross the wall and I'd 
bring you back that fallen star.

213
00:19:10.102 --> 00:19:12.138
You can't cross the wall.

214
00:19:12.471 --> 00:19:15.174
Nobody crosses the wall.
Now you're just being silly.

215
00:19:15.174 --> 00:19:18.052
I'm not being silly. I'd do it.

216
00:19:19.778 --> 00:19:21.814
For you, I'd do anything.

217
00:19:22.715 --> 00:19:24.694
My very own star.

218
00:19:28.354 --> 00:19:31.061
It seems we have ourselves
an agreement.

219
00:19:31.991 --> 00:19:35.404
You have exactly one week
or I'm marrying Humphrey.

220
00:20:47.533 --> 00:20:51.572
Mormo! Empusa! Wake up. Now!

221
00:20:52.571 --> 00:20:56.075
- What is it?
- A star has fallen.

222
00:21:03.916 --> 00:21:05.784
Where are the Babylon candles?

223
00:21:05.784 --> 00:21:10.589
You used the last one, Lamia,
200 years ago. Do you not recall?

224
00:21:10.589 --> 00:21:12.958
Perhaps we can obtain another.

225
00:21:12.958 --> 00:21:16.495
Has your mind become
as decrepit as your face, Empusa?

226
00:21:16.495 --> 00:21:19.365
You speak as if such things
are freely available.

227
00:21:19.365 --> 00:21:20.833
I know, sister, I merely thought ...

228
00:21:20.833 --> 00:21:22.468
You'd have us hunting
for a Babylon candle ...

229
00:21:22.468 --> 00:21:25.337
while some other witch finds our star.

230
00:21:25.337 --> 00:21:27.976
Fool. There's no time to waste.

231
00:21:28.674 --> 00:21:31.577
If we must retrieve it on foot,
then we shall.

232
00:21:31.577 --> 00:21:33.716
Mormo, we need information.

233
00:21:42.921 --> 00:21:47.836
If these divinations are correct,
the fallen star lies 100 miles away.

234
00:21:50.929 --> 00:21:56.402
Four centuries we've waited for this.
What hardship a few more days?

235
00:21:56.402 --> 00:21:59.872
Which of us shall go, then, 
to seek it and bring it back?

236
00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.915
- I've his kidney.
- I've his liver.

237
00:22:09.915 --> 00:22:11.655
And I've his heart.

238
00:22:14.219 --> 00:22:17.290
You'll be needing what's 
left of the last star.

239
00:22:24.229 --> 00:22:28.108
- There's not much left.
- Soon, there'll be plenty for us all.

240
00:23:24.456 --> 00:23:29.132
- Dunstan Thorn. Not again.
- It's Tristan, actually.

241
00:23:29.962 --> 00:23:34.032
Oh. You do look a 
bit like your father.

242
00:23:34.032 --> 00:23:37.603
And I suppose you intend to 
cross the wall as well, do you?

243
00:23:37.603 --> 00:23:39.538
Well, you can forget it. Go home.

244
00:23:39.538 --> 00:23:41.745
Cross the wall as well as who?

245
00:23:42.541 --> 00:23:44.943
No one. Nobody.

246
00:23:44.943 --> 00:23:47.513
Nobody crosses the wall.
You know that!

247
00:23:47.513 --> 00:23:49.882
Everybody knows that!

248
00:23:49.882 --> 00:23:53.454
Yeah ... No, no, I know.
I understand. Nobody.

249
00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:57.723
Well, I better just head
for the old homestead, then.

250
00:23:57.723 --> 00:23:58.957
Yeah.

251
00:23:58.957 --> 00:24:03.228
- Right, then. Night, Tristan.
- Good night.

252
00:24:03.228 --> 00:24:05.367
Give my best to your father.

253
00:24:28.454 --> 00:24:29.864
Off you go.

254
00:24:35.828 --> 00:24:39.264
I thought I heard you come in.
Hey, what happened? Are you hurt?

255
00:24:39.264 --> 00:24:40.599
No, I'm fine.

256
00:24:40.599 --> 00:24:42.201
- That Humphrey again?
- No.

257
00:24:42.201 --> 00:24:45.580
Actually, it was the guard.
The guard at the wall.

258
00:24:46.438 --> 00:24:47.940
Tristan, he's 97 years old.

259
00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.115
Well, that's given him plenty of
time to practice, then, hasn't it?

260
00:24:52.578 --> 00:24:55.786
Why, may I ask, were you 
trying to cross the wall?

261
00:24:58.016 --> 00:25:00.359
I might ask you the same thing.

262
00:25:50.068 --> 00:25:51.706
I have a mother.

263
00:25:53.138 --> 00:25:56.174
I mean, I have a mother.
She could still be alive.

264
00:25:56.174 --> 00:25:59.154
Oh, I hope so. 
I certainly like to think so.

265
00:26:03.048 --> 00:26:04.720
The chain you cut.

266
00:26:05.851 --> 00:26:07.286
Just like you said.

267
00:26:07.286 --> 00:26:08.457
And ...

268
00:26:11.557 --> 00:26:14.126
And the glass flower.
The flower she sold to you.

269
00:26:14.126 --> 00:26:16.228
She told me it would bring me luck.

270
00:26:16.228 --> 00:26:17.570
Thank you.

271
00:26:19.331 --> 00:26:22.539
This was also in the basket.
I've never opened it.

272
00:26:23.235 --> 00:26:25.112
It's addressed to you.

273
00:26:41.386 --> 00:26:42.621
Yep.

274
00:26:42.621 --> 00:26:47.160
"My dearest Tristan, please know that
I only ever wanted the best for you."

275
00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:52.633
Had my mistress allowed it,
I would have kept you in a heartbeat.

276
00:26:53.932 --> 00:26:56.844
My dearest wish is that
we will meet someday.

277
00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.382
The fastest way to travel
is by candlelight.

278
00:27:02.741 --> 00:27:05.687
To use it, think of me and only me.

279
00:27:06.678 --> 00:27:10.557
I will think of you 
every day, for always.

280
00:27:13.151 --> 00:27:14.652
"Your mother."

281
00:27:21.793 --> 00:27:23.465
Well, do you ... 
Do you have a light?

282
00:28:08.373 --> 00:28:09.841
Mother?

283
00:28:09.841 --> 00:28:13.178
Oh! Oh, Mother, I'm so ... 
I'm so sorry. Are you all right?

284
00:28:13.178 --> 00:28:16.488
No, I'm not. And I'm not 
your mother, so get off me!

285
00:28:17.049 --> 00:28:19.751
You're ... You're not my mother?

286
00:28:19.751 --> 00:28:22.094
Do I look like I'm your mother?

287
00:28:23.422 --> 00:28:24.491
No.

288
00:28:24.756 --> 00:28:25.962
Sorry.

289
00:28:28.193 --> 00:28:30.028
Well, are you all right?
Do you want some help?

290
00:28:30.028 --> 00:28:32.440
You can help by leaving me alone!

291
00:28:33.665 --> 00:28:35.041
All right.

292
00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.744
Oh, my God.
"Light the candle and think of me."

293
00:28:45.744 --> 00:28:47.879
I was. I was thinking of my ...

294
00:28:47.879 --> 00:28:50.655
But then Victoria and the star
just popped into ...

295
00:28:54.553 --> 00:28:56.688
Oh, excuse me, madam. Sorry.

296
00:28:56.688 --> 00:29:00.636
This may seem strange, but have 
you seen a fallen star anywhere?

297
00:29:01.326 --> 00:29:03.295
- You're funny.
- No, really, we're in a crater.

298
00:29:03.295 --> 00:29:05.468
This must be where it fell.

299
00:29:05.864 --> 00:29:07.599
Yeah, this is where it fell.

300
00:29:07.599 --> 00:29:10.669
It is. Or if you want 
to be really specific,

301
00:29:10.669 --> 00:29:13.105
up there is where this weird, 
bloody necklace ...

302
00:29:13.105 --> 00:29:15.340
came out of nowhere and knocked 
it out of the heavens ...

303
00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.275
when it was minding 
its own business.

304
00:29:17.275 --> 00:29:19.277
And over there is where it landed.

305
00:29:19.277 --> 00:29:25.091
And right here, this is where it 
got hit by a magical flying moron!

306
00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:31.496
You're the star! 
You're the star? Really?

307
00:29:32.124 --> 00:29:36.766
Oh, wow. I'm sorry.
I had no idea you'd be a ...

308
00:29:38.797 --> 00:29:41.607
May I just say in advance
that I am sorry?

309
00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:44.102
- Sorry for what?
- For this.

310
00:29:44.102 --> 00:29:47.105
Now, if I am not mistaken, this 
means you have to come with me.

311
00:29:47.105 --> 00:29:50.876
See, you're going to be a birthday 
gift for Victoria, my true love.

312
00:29:50.876 --> 00:29:52.344
But of course!

313
00:29:52.344 --> 00:29:56.383
Nothing says romance like the gift
of a kidnapped, injured woman!

314
00:29:56.681 --> 00:29:59.024
I'm not going anywhere with you.

315
00:30:04.222 --> 00:30:07.168
Hurry. You should be
on your way now, Primus.

316
00:30:08.894 --> 00:30:12.497
You must find the royal necklace
with the ruby before your brothers.

317
00:30:12.497 --> 00:30:16.945
I should like to see you take the 
throne. The first benevolent king.

318
00:30:17.569 --> 00:30:21.706
I don't doubt Stormhold would be
a better place under your rule.

319
00:30:21.706 --> 00:30:23.041
Really?

320
00:30:23.041 --> 00:30:25.644
Well, that is fascinating.

321
00:30:25.644 --> 00:30:27.879
- Don't you think, Tertius?
- Yes, indeed.

322
00:30:27.879 --> 00:30:32.725
Prince Septimus! Tertius. 
Well ... Well. Well, well, I ...

323
00:30:33.118 --> 00:30:37.327
Since you're all here,
won't you join me in a toast?

324
00:30:39.024 --> 00:30:40.759
What a very good idea.

325
00:30:40.759 --> 00:30:43.261
To the new King of Stormhold,

326
00:30:43.261 --> 00:30:46.537
whichever of you fine fellows
it might be.

327
00:30:47.232 --> 00:30:49.473
To the new King of Stormhold.

328
00:31:30.775 --> 00:31:31.912
You!

329
00:31:56.334 --> 00:31:59.007
You really thought 
that you were king?

330
00:31:59.271 --> 00:32:01.773
- You killed the Bishop?
- No, Primus.

331
00:32:01.773 --> 00:32:06.654
I think you'll find that you killed the
Bishop by drinking out of the wrong cup.

332
00:32:07.679 --> 00:32:09.714
Oh, look, when you finish wrestling
with your conscience,

333
00:32:09.714 --> 00:32:12.550
may I suggest you return
to your chamber?

334
00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:14.996
Leave the quest 
for the stone to me.

335
00:32:22.227 --> 00:32:25.071
How have we lived this way
all these years?

336
00:32:38.009 --> 00:32:42.013
In my absence, I expect you to 
make it fit for the queens we are.

337
00:32:42.647 --> 00:32:47.994
When I return with our prize,
all of us shall be young again.

338
00:32:49.688 --> 00:32:51.827
Never fear, my sisters.

339
00:32:53.291 --> 00:32:54.929
I will not fail.

340
00:33:05.337 --> 00:33:06.871
Don't you ever sleep?

341
00:33:06.871 --> 00:33:07.872
Not at night.

342
00:33:08.306 --> 00:33:09.774
May have escaped 
your notice, genius,

343
00:33:09.774 --> 00:33:11.910
but that's when stars have 
rather better things to do.

344
00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:14.312
They're coming out, shining,
that sort of thing.

345
00:33:14.312 --> 00:33:17.649
Yeah, well, it may have escaped yours,
but you're not in the sky anymore.

346
00:33:17.649 --> 00:33:19.718
Coming out is off the agenda.

347
00:33:19.718 --> 00:33:23.521
Shining has been suspended
until further notice.

348
00:33:23.521 --> 00:33:26.991
Oh, and sleeping during the day
is O-U-T.

349
00:33:27.692 --> 00:33:31.262
Unless you have some magical ability
to sleep while you're walking.

350
00:33:31.262 --> 00:33:33.732
Have you not got it
into your thick head yet?

351
00:33:33.732 --> 00:33:35.768
I'm not walking anywhere!

352
00:33:36.501 --> 00:33:37.672
Fine.

353
00:33:38.203 --> 00:33:41.274
Sit in a crater.
I've had enough of you anyway.

354
00:33:41.606 --> 00:33:44.309
I was gonna put you back in the sky
once I'd brought you to my Victoria,

355
00:33:44.309 --> 00:33:47.612
but clearly, you'd rather sit on your 
own in the middle of nowhere forever.

356
00:33:47.612 --> 00:33:51.082
And just how were you planning
to get me back to the sky?

357
00:33:51.082 --> 00:33:54.392
I find the fastest way to 
travel is by candlelight.

358
00:33:54.786 --> 00:33:58.757
- You've got a Babylon candle.
- Yeah, I have a bubbling candle.

359
00:33:58.757 --> 00:34:00.425
- A Babylon candle.
- That's what I said.

360
00:34:00.425 --> 00:34:01.526
You said "Bubbling."

361
00:34:01.526 --> 00:34:04.029
Anyway, I was going to give
what's left of it to you.

362
00:34:04.029 --> 00:34:06.464
Well, that barely has one use left.

363
00:34:06.464 --> 00:34:11.069
So be grateful I'm not using it right 
now to get us both back to the wall.

364
00:34:11.069 --> 00:34:14.482
Unless you have a better way of
getting yourself home.

365
00:34:17.075 --> 00:34:18.246
Fine!

366
00:34:18.643 --> 00:34:20.349
Help me up.

367
00:34:20.979 --> 00:34:23.425
All right. All right. 
I'm helping.

368
00:34:24.749 --> 00:34:26.451
And you are gonna have to walk
quicker than that.

369
00:34:26.451 --> 00:34:29.320
Otherwise, I'll never get you 
back to Victoria in a week.

370
00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:30.821
Don't push your luck.

371
00:34:32.657 --> 00:34:36.628
Don't take less than a florin 
for him, Bernard! You understand?

372
00:34:36.628 --> 00:34:38.863
- Yes, Mother.
- No dilly-dallying!

373
00:34:38.863 --> 00:34:42.867
And don't even think of stopping at the
tavern, Bernard, or you'll be sorry!

374
00:34:42.867 --> 00:34:45.040
A florin for your goat, boy.

375
00:34:47.906 --> 00:34:48.907
Oh.

376
00:34:50.375 --> 00:34:52.878
He's a bit small 
to pull your cart.

377
00:34:54.712 --> 00:34:56.452
You're quite right.

378
00:35:06.724 --> 00:35:08.498
That's much better.

379
00:35:13.131 --> 00:35:14.405
Bernard!

380
00:35:16.201 --> 00:35:17.475
Bernard!

381
00:35:39.657 --> 00:35:40.897
Get on!

382
00:36:17.061 --> 00:36:18.463
Who goes there?

383
00:36:18.463 --> 00:36:20.565
What do you want with me,
a poor old flower ...

384
00:36:20.565 --> 00:36:23.601
Oh, do shut up. 
I know what you are ...

385
00:36:23.601 --> 00:36:28.339
and I swear by the ordinances of the
sisterhood to which we both belong ...

386
00:36:28.339 --> 00:36:30.909
that I mean you no harm this day.

387
00:36:30.909 --> 00:36:32.945
I wish to share your meal.

388
00:36:33.344 --> 00:36:39.260
Well, one can never be too careful.
Sit down. I'll get you a seat.

389
00:36:48.159 --> 00:36:50.002
- Anything else?
- Nope.

390
00:36:53.565 --> 00:36:55.169
What's it to be?

391
00:36:55.767 --> 00:36:57.337
Heads or tails?

392
00:36:57.969 --> 00:36:59.175
Heads.

393
00:37:00.939 --> 00:37:02.713
So, stranger,

394
00:37:03.141 --> 00:37:06.244
where are you headed off to
on this fine day?

395
00:37:06.244 --> 00:37:08.223
I seek a fallen star.

396
00:37:09.247 --> 00:37:11.149
She fell not far from here.

397
00:37:11.149 --> 00:37:13.484
And when I find her,
I shall take my great knife ...

398
00:37:13.484 --> 00:37:16.054
and cut out her heart
while she still lives.

399
00:37:16.054 --> 00:37:20.559
And the glory of our youth
shall be restored.

400
00:37:21.092 --> 00:37:22.536
Fallen star?

401
00:37:23.661 --> 00:37:26.297
That's the best news I've had in ages.

402
00:37:26.297 --> 00:37:29.167
I could do with losing
a few years meself.

403
00:37:29.167 --> 00:37:31.336
So whereabouts did you ...

404
00:37:31.336 --> 00:37:32.971
Limbus grass!

405
00:37:32.971 --> 00:37:38.409
You dare to steal truth from my 
lips by feeding me limbus grass?

406
00:37:38.409 --> 00:37:42.721
Do you have any idea what a big
mistake you've made, Ditchwater Sal?

407
00:37:43.781 --> 00:37:45.984
How do you know my ...

408
00:37:45.984 --> 00:37:48.020
- Who are you?
- Look again.

409
00:37:50.521 --> 00:37:54.525
I shall not seek the star,
Your Dark Majesty. I swear.

410
00:37:54.525 --> 00:37:56.231
Seek all you wish.

411
00:37:58.363 --> 00:38:01.867
You shall not see the star, 
touch it, smell or hear it.

412
00:38:02.867 --> 00:38:06.439
You will not perceive her
even if she stands before you.

413
00:38:13.344 --> 00:38:16.086
Pray you never meet me again,
Ditchwater Sal.

414
00:38:16.981 --> 00:38:19.150
Oh, right. 
So let me get this straight.

415
00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:21.819
You think you know we're 
going the right way because ...

416
00:38:21.819 --> 00:38:24.689
And I quote, "I just do."

417
00:38:24.689 --> 00:38:26.491
I do, though. I don't know why.

418
00:38:26.491 --> 00:38:29.260
Maybe it's my love for Victoria
guiding me home.

419
00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:30.928
- Please.
- Yvonne, whether you like it or not ...

420
00:38:30.928 --> 00:38:33.665
Yvaine! My name is Yvaine, so ...

421
00:38:33.665 --> 00:38:36.067
How many times do I have to ...

422
00:38:36.067 --> 00:38:38.936
Would you ...
Would you please slow down?

423
00:38:38.936 --> 00:38:40.244
Yes, yes.

424
00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:43.408
Look, we're ...
We're going north, all right?

425
00:38:43.408 --> 00:38:44.776
The wall's north.

426
00:38:44.776 --> 00:38:48.046
And if you look up in the sky, even 
during the day, you can see the ...

427
00:38:48.046 --> 00:38:49.847
The evening star. That's so weird.

428
00:38:49.847 --> 00:38:51.816
That's funny. Hilarious.
My sides are splitting.

429
00:38:51.816 --> 00:38:53.454
No, I'm being ...

430
00:38:54.585 --> 00:38:57.827
Wait that ... That was you! Really?

431
00:38:59.057 --> 00:39:00.925
What ... What are ...

432
00:39:00.925 --> 00:39:03.361
- What are you doing?
- What does it look like I'm doing?

433
00:39:03.361 --> 00:39:05.630
- Sitting down. I'm tired.
- Please don't do this again.

434
00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:08.199
We agreed we'd stop off
at the next village to eat and rest.

435
00:39:08.199 --> 00:39:11.202
Come on, Tristan. It's midday.

436
00:39:11.769 --> 00:39:16.149
I never stay up this late!
Just, please, let me sleep!

437
00:39:16.941 --> 00:39:21.480
Okay, you ... Well, then I'll ... You 
sleep. I'll go and get something to eat.

438
00:39:28.219 --> 00:39:29.520
What are you doing?

439
00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:33.798
What does it look like I'm doing?
I'm making sure you don't run away.

440
00:39:49.874 --> 00:39:54.322
Your Highness, the soothsayer,
as you requested.

441
00:39:55.713 --> 00:39:57.351
South, you said.

442
00:39:58.282 --> 00:39:59.817
South we went.

443
00:40:01.219 --> 00:40:02.789
Still no stone.

444
00:40:04.655 --> 00:40:06.891
Do you now propose
we start swimming?

445
00:40:06.891 --> 00:40:12.063
Sire, I've merely relayed to you
what the runes have told me.

446
00:40:12.063 --> 00:40:13.701
I can do no more.

447
00:40:14.932 --> 00:40:16.934
Well, consult them again.

448
00:40:17.335 --> 00:40:18.472
Wait.

449
00:40:21.439 --> 00:40:25.478
Before we seek the stone,
I have another question.

450
00:40:27.378 --> 00:40:29.221
Am I the seventh son?

451
00:40:33.718 --> 00:40:34.919
Yes.

452
00:40:34.919 --> 00:40:36.591
Another question.

453
00:40:37.288 --> 00:40:39.961
Is my favorite color blue?

454
00:40:43.628 --> 00:40:44.729
Yes.

455
00:40:44.729 --> 00:40:48.566
Has excessive begging or pleading
ever persuaded me ...

456
00:40:48.566 --> 00:40:50.875
to spare the life of a traitor?

457
00:40:54.138 --> 00:40:55.773
What does that mean?

458
00:40:55.773 --> 00:40:58.378
- That means no.
- Good.

459
00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:02.290
Throw them again.
This time, throw them high.

460
00:41:06.317 --> 00:41:08.421
Do you work for my brother?

461
00:41:24.802 --> 00:41:27.873
So, do we continue west?

462
00:41:58.302 --> 00:41:59.542
Tristan?

463
00:42:08.513 --> 00:42:09.957
Who's there?

464
00:42:17.388 --> 00:42:19.197
Tristan, is that you?

465
00:42:20.258 --> 00:42:21.793
It's not funny.

466
00:42:26.097 --> 00:42:27.371
Tristan?

467
00:42:37.508 --> 00:42:38.782
Come on.

468
00:43:22.119 --> 00:43:24.822
Be careful how much magic
you use, sister.

469
00:43:24.822 --> 00:43:26.724
It's beginning to show.

470
00:43:26.724 --> 00:43:30.161
One goat and a small enchantment.
Hardly extravagant.

471
00:43:30.161 --> 00:43:33.197
Well, even using the ring
will take its toll.

472
00:43:33.197 --> 00:43:35.633
Better you call 
on us only in dire need.

473
00:43:35.633 --> 00:43:39.036
And use your runes
to locate the star yourself.

474
00:43:39.036 --> 00:43:41.138
I used them and she should be here.

475
00:43:41.138 --> 00:43:43.982
But now they're just 
telling me gibberish.

476
00:44:01.392 --> 00:44:04.295
It is because you must stay
where you are, my sister.

477
00:44:04.295 --> 00:44:05.630
She is coming to you.

478
00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:08.366
Oh, be warned, Lamia.
Delicacy is needed.

479
00:44:08.366 --> 00:44:12.503
Misery has drained her.
She's barely shining.

480
00:44:12.503 --> 00:44:17.281
Set a trap that ensures her heart
is glowing before you cut it out.

481
00:44:18.075 --> 00:44:21.283
Yvaine! Yvaine!

482
00:44:22.113 --> 00:44:23.648
Oh, you idiot.

483
00:44:45.369 --> 00:44:48.042
You shall become human.

484
00:46:05.049 --> 00:46:09.353
You are Billy, the innkeeper.
I am your wife, and you're our daughter.

485
00:46:09.353 --> 00:46:13.858
Now, make everything ready.
Our special guest will be here soon.

486
00:46:24.735 --> 00:46:28.939
I mean, who's to say he'd have even
kept his promise about the candle?

487
00:46:28.939 --> 00:46:32.710
I just refuse to believe he's the 
only person in Stormhold ...

488
00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:34.512
who could've helped me.

489
00:46:34.512 --> 00:46:38.824
Going on and on and on. Just 
"Victoria this" and "Victoria that."

490
00:46:41.185 --> 00:46:42.459
Tristan?

491
00:46:43.854 --> 00:46:46.493
Please protect our sister, Tristan.

492
00:46:47.491 --> 00:46:50.369
Yvaine is in grave danger.

493
00:46:51.162 --> 00:46:53.505
The unicorn came to help her.

494
00:46:54.198 --> 00:46:56.735
But now they're heading into a trap.

495
00:47:00.704 --> 00:47:03.548
No star is safe in Stormhold.

496
00:47:04.441 --> 00:47:08.846
The last to fall, 400 years ago,

497
00:47:08.846 --> 00:47:14.227
was captured by the same witches
who seek Yvaine now.

498
00:47:15.386 --> 00:47:17.092
They tricked her,

499
00:47:18.589 --> 00:47:20.124
cared for her,

500
00:47:21.392 --> 00:47:25.067
and when her heart
was once more aglow,

501
00:47:25.629 --> 00:47:29.042
they cut it from her chest ...

502
00:47:30.067 --> 00:47:31.739
and ate it.

503
00:47:34.271 --> 00:47:36.340
There's no time to waste.

504
00:47:36.340 --> 00:47:38.175
A coach is coming.

505
00:47:38.175 --> 00:47:41.850
By any means possible,
you must get on it.

506
00:47:42.446 --> 00:47:43.549
Run.

507
00:48:14.445 --> 00:48:15.582
Whoa!

508
00:48:24.021 --> 00:48:27.124
If Septimus insists on sending 
a boy to do a man's job ...

509
00:48:27.124 --> 00:48:28.225
No, no, no, wait, please!

510
00:48:28.225 --> 00:48:30.194
I don't know a Septimus.
I just need a lift.

511
00:48:30.194 --> 00:48:33.038
Look at me, look at me.
I'm unarmed. Please.

512
00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:35.599
Please, let me ride with you.

513
00:48:35.599 --> 00:48:39.069
I'm afraid that's impossible. I'm 
on a quest of enormous importance.

514
00:48:39.069 --> 00:48:41.071
Well, then all the more reason
to take me with you.

515
00:48:41.071 --> 00:48:44.675
There may come a time when you 
need a second pair of hands. Sir?

516
00:48:44.675 --> 00:48:48.350
Please. Maybe providence sent me
to you just as it sent you to me.

517
00:48:49.747 --> 00:48:52.489
- Get on.
- Oh, yes. Thank you.

518
00:49:17.441 --> 00:49:21.013
Goodness me, my dear. Come in 
out of this wretched rain!

519
00:49:21.412 --> 00:49:25.690
We have food and drink, a warm bed
and plenty of hot water for a bath.

520
00:49:44.735 --> 00:49:48.944
How do you like your bath?
Warm, hot or boil-a-lobster?

521
00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:51.141
I honestly don't know.

522
00:49:51.141 --> 00:49:52.643
Then let me choose for you,

523
00:49:52.643 --> 00:49:55.885
and I'll have my husband
take your horse to the stable.

524
00:49:56.814 --> 00:49:57.985
Billy?

525
00:50:08.826 --> 00:50:12.136
Now, let's get you out
of your wet things, shall we?

526
00:50:23.407 --> 00:50:24.975
Feeling better?

527
00:50:24.975 --> 00:50:26.749
Much. Thank you.

528
00:50:27.111 --> 00:50:29.713
The warm water's actually 
done me a world of good.

529
00:50:29.713 --> 00:50:32.783
You see?
The powers of a nice, hot bath.

530
00:50:32.783 --> 00:50:35.263
And your leg? Any improvement?

531
00:50:37.121 --> 00:50:38.998
That is extraordinary.

532
00:50:39.456 --> 00:50:43.293
It's the very least I could do.
I'm just glad you're feeling better.

533
00:50:43.293 --> 00:50:45.262
You seem happier in yourself, too.

534
00:50:45.262 --> 00:50:48.607
I do feel happier. Less troubled.

535
00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:51.006
Wonderful.

536
00:50:51.368 --> 00:50:55.008
Nothing like a nice soak
to warm the cockles of your heart.

537
00:51:02.946 --> 00:51:06.383
Now, I'm only a 
simple innkeeper's wife,

538
00:51:06.383 --> 00:51:09.921
but I've been told
I have a healer's hands.

539
00:51:11.021 --> 00:51:13.501
I'd be glad to give you a massage.

540
00:51:13.991 --> 00:51:15.592
What's a massage?

541
00:51:15.592 --> 00:51:18.868
Never had ... Well, bless my soul.

542
00:51:19.563 --> 00:51:23.801
Nothing like a massage to send you off
for the finest and deepest night's sleep.

543
00:51:23.801 --> 00:51:25.369
I do have trouble sleeping at night.

544
00:51:25.369 --> 00:51:27.280
Lie on your back, dear.

545
00:51:30.274 --> 00:51:32.276
Why not close your eyes?

546
00:51:33.310 --> 00:51:35.722
You'll drift off better that way.

547
00:51:50.060 --> 00:51:51.129
Hello!

548
00:51:53.197 --> 00:51:54.264
Service!

549
00:51:54.264 --> 00:51:56.900
Maybe we should carry on
and try the next inn,

550
00:51:56.900 --> 00:51:59.736
especially if this stone is 
as close as your runes say.

551
00:51:59.736 --> 00:52:01.510
I'll give it one more try.

552
00:52:06.009 --> 00:52:07.344
Relax here, my love.

553
00:52:07.344 --> 00:52:10.757
I'll be back just as soon as
I've taken care of this customer.

554
00:52:15.819 --> 00:52:18.355
At last. We require accommodation.

555
00:52:18.355 --> 00:52:21.734
Please help my friend
take the horses to the stables.

556
00:52:35.672 --> 00:52:36.843
Hello!

557
00:52:39.676 --> 00:52:40.677
Oh.

558
00:52:57.427 --> 00:52:59.029
I'm accustomed to better service,

559
00:52:59.029 --> 00:53:01.941
but you're awake now
and that's what counts.

560
00:53:02.766 --> 00:53:04.201
Prepare your best room.

561
00:53:04.201 --> 00:53:05.369
- She got the stone.
- Stone, stone.

562
00:53:05.369 --> 00:53:09.640
I'll thank you not to bother my 
guest, sir. I am the lady of this inn.

563
00:53:09.640 --> 00:53:11.275
Glass of wine?

564
00:53:11.275 --> 00:53:12.515
No, no, no, no, no.

565
00:53:13.377 --> 00:53:14.412
No.

566
00:53:15.345 --> 00:53:19.418
Until my brother is dead, I have 
vowed to drink only my own wine.

567
00:53:21.518 --> 00:53:25.056
Though my friend in the stables
might be glad of a drop.

568
00:53:27.324 --> 00:53:29.303
Your best room, perhaps?

569
00:53:30.861 --> 00:53:32.237
Of course.

570
00:53:39.403 --> 00:53:41.576
I'm sorry. I presumed that ...

571
00:53:42.105 --> 00:53:44.107
Traveling alone, are you?

572
00:53:45.576 --> 00:53:49.046
I've just stabled my four black 
stallions and my carriage.

573
00:53:49.046 --> 00:53:52.220
Well, I say mine.
It belonged to my late father.

574
00:54:06.163 --> 00:54:07.497
Thank you.

575
00:54:07.497 --> 00:54:11.902
That's so kind, thank you very much.
My name's Tristan. What's yours?

576
00:54:11.902 --> 00:54:13.176
Bernard.

577
00:54:20.944 --> 00:54:24.181
There was not a horse or beast
he could not master.

578
00:54:24.181 --> 00:54:27.017
So much so that in his youth,
he took to riding a camel,

579
00:54:27.017 --> 00:54:28.485
which was comical.

580
00:54:28.485 --> 00:54:30.687
When he passed,
the carriage came to me.

581
00:54:30.687 --> 00:54:31.822
She's got the stone!

582
00:54:31.822 --> 00:54:32.923
- She's got it!
- She's got it!

583
00:54:32.923 --> 00:54:36.293
And it's the largest in all 
of Stormhold, so they say.

584
00:54:36.293 --> 00:54:39.162
How nice for you. 
If you'll excuse me.

585
00:54:39.162 --> 00:54:40.299
Wait.

586
00:54:40.964 --> 00:54:43.066
That stone you're wearing. 
It can't be.

587
00:54:43.066 --> 00:54:44.768
- Oh, finally.
- Yeah, good, well done, yes, sir.

588
00:54:44.768 --> 00:54:45.836
- Well done.
- Sweet.

589
00:54:45.836 --> 00:54:46.871
Come here. Let me see it.

590
00:54:59.883 --> 00:55:01.985
You have no idea
what you're meddling with.

591
00:55:01.985 --> 00:55:07.891
I am Primus, the first born of Stormhold
and I demand that you bring it to me!

592
00:55:07.891 --> 00:55:09.493
Bring me the stone! Now!

593
00:55:09.493 --> 00:55:12.667
Prince Primus! Don't touch anything
they give you! They tried to ...

594
00:55:28.679 --> 00:55:30.954
- Are you all right?
- Billy!

595
00:55:33.083 --> 00:55:34.254
Get him!

596
00:56:12.823 --> 00:56:15.325
The burning golden heart
of a star at peace ...

597
00:56:15.325 --> 00:56:18.462
is so much better than your 
frightened little heart.

598
00:56:18.462 --> 00:56:22.432
Even so, better 
than no heart at all.

599
00:56:24.401 --> 00:56:27.143
Yvaine?
Hold me tight and think of home.

600
00:56:35.278 --> 00:56:37.985
No!

601
00:56:56.400 --> 00:57:00.904
- What the hell did you do?
- What did I do? What did you do?

602
00:57:00.904 --> 00:57:03.907
"Think of home!" 
That was a great plan!

603
00:57:03.907 --> 00:57:06.076
You thought of your home
and I thought of mine ...

604
00:57:06.076 --> 00:57:08.044
and now we're half-way 
between the two!

605
00:57:08.044 --> 00:57:11.214
Oh, you stupid cow! What did 
you think of your home for?

606
00:57:11.214 --> 00:57:12.749
You just said "home"!

607
00:57:12.749 --> 00:57:16.186
If you wanted me to think of 
your home, you should have said!

608
00:57:16.186 --> 00:57:18.722
Some crazy lady
was going to cut your heart out ...

609
00:57:18.722 --> 00:57:20.557
and you wanted
more specific instructions?

610
00:57:20.557 --> 00:57:24.004
Perhaps you'd like it in writing!
Or a diagram, maybe!

611
00:57:32.335 --> 00:57:36.180
Look, Captain Shakespeare!
Caught ourselves a little bonus!

612
00:57:36.740 --> 00:57:39.083
A couple of Lightning Marshals!

613
00:57:42.913 --> 00:57:46.082
They don't look
like Lightning Marshals to me.

614
00:57:46.082 --> 00:57:49.286
Why else would anyone be up here
in the middle of a storm?

615
00:57:49.286 --> 00:57:52.622
"Why else would anyone be up here
in the middle of a storm?"

616
00:57:52.622 --> 00:57:54.458
Well, let's think.

617
00:57:54.458 --> 00:57:57.768
Maybe for the same god-forsaken 
reason we are!

618
00:57:58.462 --> 00:58:00.066
Now, who are you?

619
00:58:01.898 --> 00:58:05.235
Let's see if a night on our lovely 
brig will loosen their lips!

620
00:58:05.235 --> 00:58:08.271
- Get them in the brig!
- You heard the man! Let's go!

621
00:58:08.271 --> 00:58:13.143
Get them into the brig and the rest 
of you dirty dogs, back to work!

622
00:58:13.143 --> 00:58:15.782
We've got lightning to catch! 
Come on!

623
00:58:39.736 --> 00:58:43.547
- They're going to kill us, aren't they?
- I don't know.

624
00:58:46.176 --> 00:58:49.088
You know, it's funny. 
I used to watch ...

625
00:58:50.447 --> 00:58:53.223
I used to watch people
having adventures.

626
00:58:55.285 --> 00:58:56.820
I envied them.

627
00:58:58.288 --> 00:59:01.358
You ever heard the expression,
"Be careful what you wish for"?

628
00:59:01.358 --> 00:59:04.160
What? So ending up with my heart cut 
out, that'll serve me right, will it?

629
00:59:04.160 --> 00:59:07.231
No. No, I didn't ...
I didn't mean it like that.

630
00:59:09.199 --> 00:59:11.372
Look, I admire you dreaming.

631
00:59:12.736 --> 00:59:14.804
Shop boy like me,

632
00:59:14.804 --> 00:59:16.806
I could never have imagined
an adventure this big ...

633
00:59:16.806 --> 00:59:19.115
in order to have wished for it.

634
00:59:19.442 --> 00:59:22.479
I just thought I'd find some 
lump of celestial rock ...

635
00:59:22.479 --> 00:59:25.181
and take it home, 
and that would be it.

636
00:59:25.181 --> 00:59:26.557
And you got me.

637
00:59:34.824 --> 00:59:38.962
If there's one thing I've learned
in all my years watching Earth,

638
00:59:38.962 --> 00:59:41.806
it's that people aren't
what they may seem.

639
00:59:42.499 --> 00:59:43.633
There are shop boys ...

640
00:59:43.633 --> 00:59:48.004
and there are boys who just happen
to work in shops for the time being.

641
00:59:48.004 --> 00:59:52.043
And trust me, Tristan,
you're no shop boy.

642
00:59:54.244 --> 00:59:56.951
You saved my life. Thank you.

643
01:00:01.885 --> 01:00:03.523
Well, well, well.

644
01:00:04.120 --> 01:00:05.997
The last brother dead.

645
01:00:07.691 --> 01:00:09.761
Which means that I'm king.

646
01:00:13.797 --> 01:00:15.071
I'm king!

647
01:00:18.001 --> 01:00:19.639
Not yet, brother.

648
01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:23.111
Damn. I still need the stone.

649
01:00:24.307 --> 01:00:28.118
- Your brother doesn't have it?
- Well, why don't you find out?

650
01:00:38.321 --> 01:00:40.056
Where is my stone?

651
01:00:40.056 --> 01:00:41.891
I don't ...

652
01:00:41.891 --> 01:00:44.894
The man, see, your brother ...
I heard him speak of a stone.

653
01:00:44.894 --> 01:00:47.897
Yeah. The girl had it. 
The girl had it.

654
01:00:47.897 --> 01:00:49.899
- What girl?
- I don't know.

655
01:00:49.899 --> 01:00:52.702
A girl. She got away. Because 
this was a trap set up for her,

656
01:00:52.702 --> 01:00:55.872
but your brother,
he come just straight into it.

657
01:00:55.872 --> 01:00:58.181
A trap? Set by who?

658
01:00:58.441 --> 01:01:00.443
A woman you should pray
you'll never meet.

659
01:01:00.443 --> 01:01:01.683
She's ...

660
01:01:02.612 --> 01:01:05.582
She's gone. She took 
your brother's carriage.

661
01:01:05.582 --> 01:01:07.784
This woman wanted my stone?

662
01:01:07.784 --> 01:01:10.286
No. She wanted the girl's heart.

663
01:01:10.286 --> 01:01:14.632
She said the girl was a star and she 
wanted to cut out her heart and ...

664
01:01:17.227 --> 01:01:18.467
Eat it?

665
01:01:20.196 --> 01:01:21.606
Oh, my God.

666
01:01:23.133 --> 01:01:25.738
Do you have any 
idea what this means?

667
01:01:27.003 --> 01:01:28.675
Everlasting life.

668
01:01:30.006 --> 01:01:32.042
King forever.

669
01:01:36.146 --> 01:01:37.814
It's not here, sire.

670
01:01:37.814 --> 01:01:39.953
This idiot's coming with us.

671
01:02:04.340 --> 01:02:06.509
- Ask again.
- We have asked again ...

672
01:02:06.509 --> 01:02:08.912
and the answer is still the same.
She is airborne!

673
01:02:08.912 --> 01:02:10.313
Well, she can't 
remain so forever.

674
01:02:10.313 --> 01:02:12.382
Inform me
as soon as she touches ground!

675
01:02:12.382 --> 01:02:14.384
Immediately! Do you understand?

676
01:02:14.384 --> 01:02:18.054
Watch your tongue, sister!
It is you and not we who've lost her.

677
01:02:18.054 --> 01:02:20.023
Lost her and broken the knife!

678
01:02:20.023 --> 01:02:23.093
Even if you apprehend her,
how will you complete the deed?

679
01:02:23.093 --> 01:02:26.563
Perhaps you should return now
and one of us set out in your place.

680
01:02:26.563 --> 01:02:27.864
Don't be absurd.

681
01:02:27.864 --> 01:02:30.834
I'll bring her home
and deal with her there.

682
01:02:30.834 --> 01:02:33.746
Be sure everything is ready
for our arrival.

683
01:02:40.543 --> 01:02:42.716
Tell me about Victoria then.

684
01:02:46.316 --> 01:02:47.988
Well, she ...

685
01:02:51.554 --> 01:02:54.257
There's nothing more to tell you.

686
01:02:54.257 --> 01:02:59.832
Because the little I know about 
love is that it's unconditional.

687
01:03:00.530 --> 01:03:03.032
It's not something you can buy.

688
01:03:03.032 --> 01:03:05.944
Hang on. This wasn't 
about me buying her love.

689
01:03:08.004 --> 01:03:10.814
This was a way for me
to prove how I felt.

690
01:03:13.543 --> 01:03:16.613
And what's she doing to prove
how she feels about you?

691
01:03:16.613 --> 01:03:17.819
Well ...

692
01:03:22.185 --> 01:03:26.030
Look, Yvaine, you'll understand
when you meet her. All right?

693
01:03:26.489 --> 01:03:29.595
Provided we don't get murdered
by pirates first.

694
01:03:30.593 --> 01:03:35.799
Murdered by pirates. Heart torn 
out and eaten. Meet Victoria.

695
01:03:35.799 --> 01:03:38.643
I can't quite decide
which sounds more fun.

696
01:03:41.137 --> 01:03:43.473
We've located the sky vessel.

697
01:03:43.473 --> 01:03:46.176
It's headed north for the port 
town on Mount Drummond ...

698
01:03:46.176 --> 01:03:49.612
and you are no longer
the only one seeking the star.

699
01:03:49.612 --> 01:03:51.681
There's someone 
following your tracks!

700
01:03:51.681 --> 01:03:53.883
A witch? A warlock?

701
01:03:53.883 --> 01:03:57.057
A prince and he's catching 
you up! Get a move on!

702
01:04:24.514 --> 01:04:30.862
So, this is the part where you tell me
who you are and why you're up here.

703
01:04:31.621 --> 01:04:36.900
Or I'll snap her pretty little 
fingers one by one like dry twigs!

704
01:04:39.162 --> 01:04:41.232
My name is Tristan Thorn.

705
01:04:41.497 --> 01:04:44.068
- This is my wife, Yvaine.
- Your wife?

706
01:04:44.701 --> 01:04:48.444
Far too young and radiant
to belong to just one man!

707
01:04:48.771 --> 01:04:52.684
It's share and share alike
aboard my vessel, sonny boy!

708
01:04:54.177 --> 01:04:55.278
If you dare even touch her ...

709
01:04:55.278 --> 01:04:58.081
You may think you're showing a little 
spirit in front of your lady friend,

710
01:04:58.081 --> 01:05:01.017
but if you talk back to me again,
I'll feed your tongue to the dogs,

711
01:05:01.017 --> 01:05:03.121
you impertinent little pup!

712
01:05:03.987 --> 01:05:06.956
- Sir?
- Better! But still interrupting.

713
01:05:06.956 --> 01:05:10.894
Let's see. A hanging's 
always good for morale!

714
01:05:10.894 --> 01:05:13.431
Maybe we'll watch you dance
a gallows jig!

715
01:05:15.465 --> 01:05:19.469
Or perhaps I'll just tip you over 
the side and have done with it!

716
01:05:20.169 --> 01:05:22.171
It's a very long way down.

717
01:05:22.171 --> 01:05:25.675
Plenty of time to reminisce
about your pitifully short lives.

718
01:05:25.675 --> 01:05:28.711
Please, look, we're just 
trying to make our way home,

719
01:05:28.711 --> 01:05:30.146
back to a place called Wall,
where I come from.

720
01:05:30.146 --> 01:05:31.781
What did you say?

721
01:05:31.781 --> 01:05:33.983
I said we were trying to 
get home to Wall.

722
01:05:33.983 --> 01:05:35.985
That's one lie too many, my son.

723
01:05:35.985 --> 01:05:37.053
- Wall?
- Shut up.

724
01:05:37.053 --> 01:05:38.121
What's he saying?

725
01:05:38.121 --> 01:05:41.124
Thought you could just wander
onto my patch, did you?

726
01:05:41.124 --> 01:05:42.659
- He is. Yeah, he's gonna ...
- And live to tell the tale?

727
01:05:42.659 --> 01:05:45.261
All right, go, on the deck, on the 
deck, on the deck! Now, go, go, go!

728
01:05:45.261 --> 01:05:49.732
Big mistake, Mr. Thorn!
And the last one you'll ever make!

729
01:05:56.973 --> 01:05:59.943
- No! No! You brute!
- Come ... Come on!

730
01:05:59.943 --> 01:06:03.117
- You murderer! You pig!
- Come on! Get up! Get up!

731
01:06:04.514 --> 01:06:06.883
I'm taking the girl to my cabin,
and mark my words,

732
01:06:06.883 --> 01:06:08.718
anyone who disturbs me
for the next few hours ...

733
01:06:08.718 --> 01:06:10.687
will get the same treatment!
- What? You'll ...

734
01:06:10.687 --> 01:06:13.423
No, you idiot! I'll sling you 
over the side as well!

735
01:06:13.423 --> 01:06:14.731
Oh, yeah.

736
01:06:19.028 --> 01:06:21.440
Captain's busy. So should you be.

737
01:06:23.032 --> 01:06:24.841
Get in there, wench!

738
01:06:30.873 --> 01:06:33.546
So, that went well, I thought.

739
01:06:34.344 --> 01:06:38.715
Now, tell me news
of my beloved England.

740
01:06:38.715 --> 01:06:41.354
I want to hear 
absolutely everything.

741
01:06:42.618 --> 01:06:45.722
Hang on. I can't believe your 
crew fell for that.

742
01:06:45.722 --> 01:06:48.257
And where in God's name
did you get that mannequin from?

743
01:06:48.257 --> 01:06:50.059
Oh, it works every time.

744
01:06:50.059 --> 01:06:52.395
An ounce of bargaining,
a pinch of trickery,

745
01:06:52.395 --> 01:06:55.031
a soupson of intimidation, 
et voilà!

746
01:06:55.031 --> 01:06:57.233
The perfect recipe
for a towering reputation ...

747
01:06:57.233 --> 01:06:59.802
without ever having to spill
one drop of blood.

748
01:06:59.802 --> 01:07:03.306
Ever try to get blood stains out
of a silk shirt? Nightmare.

749
01:07:03.306 --> 01:07:06.876
Right. I still don't understand
how they won't recognize me.

750
01:07:06.876 --> 01:07:07.977
Tristan, dear boy, when I'm done,

751
01:07:07.977 --> 01:07:09.045
your own mother won't recognize you.

752
01:07:09.045 --> 01:07:10.146
Now, we've no time to waste.

753
01:07:10.146 --> 01:07:12.115
We have only two hours
before we make port.

754
01:07:12.115 --> 01:07:13.855
First and foremost ...

755
01:07:17.487 --> 01:07:21.190
It'll be so good to see you
out of those dreary clothes.

756
01:07:21.190 --> 01:07:24.500
So very small-town errand-boy.
Howlingly parochial.

757
01:07:26.195 --> 01:07:28.436
Here. Tres you.

758
01:07:29.032 --> 01:07:31.801
I wore it as a younger man.
I hate to throw anything away.

759
01:07:31.801 --> 01:07:33.503
You know the day you do,
it'll come back in fashion,

760
01:07:33.503 --> 01:07:35.505
be oh, so de la mode.

761
01:07:35.505 --> 01:07:37.006
Now, you, darling.

762
01:07:37.006 --> 01:07:38.808
I have some lovely dresses.
Take your pick.

763
01:07:38.808 --> 01:07:40.376
Oh, no, really, I'm fine.

764
01:07:40.376 --> 01:07:42.788
Honey, you're wearing a bathrobe.

765
01:07:44.714 --> 01:07:48.423
Now, England, England.
I want to hear everything.

766
01:07:49.118 --> 01:07:51.421
- You're not from England.
- Oh, no, sadly, no.

767
01:07:51.421 --> 01:07:53.823
But from my earliest youth,
I lapped up the stories.

768
01:07:53.823 --> 01:07:55.658
People always told me they were
nothing more than folklore,

769
01:07:55.658 --> 01:07:58.261
but my heart told 
me they were true.

770
01:07:58.261 --> 01:08:00.563
As a boy, I'd scurry away
from my father at the market ...

771
01:08:00.563 --> 01:08:03.533
while he did his deals
just to peek over the wall,

772
01:08:03.533 --> 01:08:06.636
dream of, perhaps, crossing it 
one day, seeing England for myself.

773
01:08:06.636 --> 01:08:09.138
Really? So you ...
You were here looking over there.

774
01:08:09.138 --> 01:08:10.412
Oh, yes.

775
01:08:11.908 --> 01:08:14.320
- Hair.
- Hair?

776
01:08:15.478 --> 01:08:17.880
Mind you, I did 
my best to fit in.

777
01:08:17.880 --> 01:08:20.249
Tried to make my father,
Captain Ghostmaker, proud.

778
01:08:20.249 --> 01:08:22.752
Forged a decent reputation
as a ruthless marauder ...

779
01:08:22.752 --> 01:08:24.754
and cold-blooded killer.

780
01:08:25.121 --> 01:08:26.895
But my father died.

781
01:08:27.657 --> 01:08:29.959
I always promised him I'd 
take over the family business,

782
01:08:29.959 --> 01:08:31.961
keep the old girl flying.

783
01:08:32.962 --> 01:08:34.964
You have no idea the lightness
it brings to my heart ...

784
01:08:34.964 --> 01:08:38.101
being able to confide in you
charming young people.

785
01:08:38.101 --> 01:08:40.536
The pressure of maintaining the
whole Captain Shakespeare persona ...

786
01:08:40.536 --> 01:08:42.638
for the sake of the crew, 
I don't know.

787
01:08:42.638 --> 01:08:44.273
Sometimes ...

788
01:08:44.273 --> 01:08:47.343
You see, I'm very much
a man of my own creation.

789
01:08:47.343 --> 01:08:50.313
Even chose the name specially.
Took me ages.

790
01:08:50.313 --> 01:08:52.515
See, I'm thinking
legendary British wordsmith.

791
01:08:52.515 --> 01:08:55.586
My enemies and crew are thinking,
"Shake! Spear!"

792
01:08:56.185 --> 01:08:58.392
It's little things 
like that make me happy.

793
01:08:58.688 --> 01:09:00.857
I don't understand that.

794
01:09:00.857 --> 01:09:03.759
Surely it would make you happier
just to be yourself.

795
01:09:03.759 --> 01:09:07.163
Why fight to be accepted by people
you don't actually want to be like?

796
01:09:07.163 --> 01:09:10.234
Yeah.
Why would anyone do that to himself?

797
01:09:11.501 --> 01:09:12.775
Exactly.

798
01:09:15.838 --> 01:09:19.717
Port ahoy! 
Ready the lightning barrels!

799
01:09:33.456 --> 01:09:34.764
Hurry up.

800
01:09:43.766 --> 01:09:46.769
Yeah. Doesn't seem very fresh.
I'll be honest.

801
01:09:46.769 --> 01:09:48.905
Shall I give you a little taste,
young Ferdinand?

802
01:09:48.905 --> 01:09:50.714
No, no. Oh, there you go.

803
01:09:53.276 --> 01:09:55.111
Brilliant. Like they're cheap.

804
01:09:55.111 --> 01:09:59.081
I think it's still crackling,
very much alive, still tray-fresh.

805
01:09:59.348 --> 01:10:02.051
- So, name your best price.
- For 10,000 bolts?

806
01:10:02.051 --> 01:10:04.587
Ten thousand bolts
of finest quality grade "A".

807
01:10:04.587 --> 01:10:07.557
Yeah, but it's difficult to shift,
isn't it? Difficult to store.

808
01:10:07.557 --> 01:10:10.893
If I get the Revenue Men in here
sniffing round, what's the ...

809
01:10:10.893 --> 01:10:12.762
Best price, 150 guineas.

810
01:10:12.762 --> 01:10:15.198
Gentlemen, put the merchandise
back onboard and prepare to sail.

811
01:10:15.198 --> 01:10:16.632
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.
- Ferdinand, always a pleasure.

812
01:10:16.632 --> 01:10:18.467
Hold on. Hold on. One minute.
Hold on, cuddles.

813
01:10:18.467 --> 01:10:20.436
- Oh, he's ...
- One-sixty. One-sixty.

814
01:10:20.436 --> 01:10:22.471
Seeing as I'm feeling
particularly generous today,

815
01:10:22.471 --> 01:10:23.839
I'll settle for 200.

816
01:10:23.839 --> 01:10:26.976
Two hundred? Okay.
You're having a laugh.

817
01:10:26.976 --> 01:10:28.344
Have you had your head in that?

818
01:10:28.344 --> 01:10:31.450
Has he been sailing up
where the air's too thin?

819
01:10:32.915 --> 01:10:35.554
- You're being very rude.
- Not anymore.

820
01:10:36.686 --> 01:10:38.554
- Two hundred.
- One-eighty.

821
01:10:38.554 --> 01:10:40.089
- Two hundred.
- That's not negotiation.

822
01:10:40.089 --> 01:10:42.491
I'm changing my number.
One-eight-five.

823
01:10:42.491 --> 01:10:45.027
- Did I hear 200?
- From you, you did. Yeah.

824
01:10:45.027 --> 01:10:47.930
- You said 200.
- If I did, you're a ventriloquist.

825
01:10:47.930 --> 01:10:50.034
Okay, one-nine-five. Final offer.

826
01:10:50.366 --> 01:10:54.537
One-nine-five it is. So, with 
sales tax, that's, let's see, 200.

827
01:10:54.537 --> 01:10:56.778
Brilliant. Put it in the back.

828
01:10:57.139 --> 01:10:57.707
Unbelievable. Here.

829
01:10:57.707 --> 01:10:59.345
Unbelievable. Here.

830
01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:06.554
Yes? Can I help you?

831
01:11:08.551 --> 01:11:09.688
Nosy.

832
01:11:10.119 --> 01:11:13.456
Have you heard any of these rumors
going round about a fallen star?

833
01:11:13.456 --> 01:11:14.957
Everyone's talking about it.

834
01:11:14.957 --> 01:11:18.361
You get your hands on one of them,
we can shut up shop. Retire.

835
01:11:18.361 --> 01:11:20.101
- Fallen star?
- Yeah.

836
01:11:26.102 --> 01:11:27.903
- Nothing on your travels?
- No.

837
01:11:27.903 --> 01:11:29.238
Not even a little 
sniff of a whisper?

838
01:11:29.238 --> 01:11:31.040
Everyone's going on about it
down at the market.

839
01:11:31.040 --> 01:11:32.408
Which market?
The market near the wall?

840
01:11:32.408 --> 01:11:33.476
Yeah.

841
01:11:33.476 --> 01:11:35.344
Well, Ferdy, you're wasting your 
time listening to gossip ...

842
01:11:35.344 --> 01:11:37.380
from the kind of pond scum
trading down there.

843
01:11:37.380 --> 01:11:39.815
- Well, if it ...
- Oh, my word! Speak of the devil.

844
01:11:39.815 --> 01:11:41.651
Oh, yeah? What were 
you saying, then?

845
01:11:41.651 --> 01:11:44.053
Oh, what a wonderful woman
you are, Sal.

846
01:11:44.053 --> 01:11:45.855
How the world wouldn't be
the same place without you.

847
01:11:45.855 --> 01:11:47.623
You look great. You've had 
your feet done, haven't you?

848
01:11:47.623 --> 01:11:51.827
But, you two, you have business
to attend to. Sal. Ferdy. Good day.

849
01:11:51.827 --> 01:11:54.569
Come on.
I've got something new for you.

850
01:12:01.203 --> 01:12:02.977
Captain Shakespeare.

851
01:12:03.339 --> 01:12:04.740
Stand down!

852
01:12:04.740 --> 01:12:06.542
Meet my nephew,
the fearsome buccaneer,

853
01:12:06.542 --> 01:12:08.077
Tristan Thorn.

854
01:12:08.377 --> 01:12:11.013
He'll be joining us 
for our journey home.

855
01:12:11.013 --> 01:12:14.460
I have the perfect gift
to keep you amused on the way.

856
01:12:21.290 --> 01:12:22.359
Oh ...

857
01:12:28.631 --> 01:12:30.199
Right, you lazy dogs!

858
01:12:30.199 --> 01:12:32.906
Let's get young Tristan
on his way home!

859
01:14:25.047 --> 01:14:26.287
Touché.

860
01:14:26.916 --> 01:14:28.451
I'm impressed.

861
01:14:44.366 --> 01:14:46.505
Yvaine, I know what you are.

862
01:14:48.804 --> 01:14:51.841
No, no. Have no fear.

863
01:14:52.308 --> 01:14:56.517
No one on this vessel will harm you,
but there are plenty who would.

864
01:14:59.882 --> 01:15:03.719
Your emotions give you away, Yvaine.
You must learn to control them.

865
01:15:03.719 --> 01:15:06.121
You've been glowing
more brightly every day,

866
01:15:06.121 --> 01:15:07.656
and I think you know why.

867
01:15:07.656 --> 01:15:10.466
Of course I know why I'm glowing.
I'm a star.

868
01:15:10.593 --> 01:15:14.597
- And what do stars do best?
- Well, it's certainly not the waltz.

869
01:15:25.741 --> 01:15:27.049
- Now try.
- Okay.

870
01:15:30.479 --> 01:15:32.253
- See?
- Very good.

871
01:15:47.496 --> 01:15:48.998
Due west, you say?

872
01:15:48.998 --> 01:15:50.432
And you're certain
he had a girl with him?

873
01:15:50.432 --> 01:15:51.534
Yeah.

874
01:15:51.534 --> 01:15:54.879
You're sure? Absolutely sure?
Sure you're not lying?

875
01:15:55.337 --> 01:15:57.680
I'd cross my heart if I had one.

876
01:15:58.974 --> 01:16:00.709
- Brilliant.
- You'd better be telling the truth,

877
01:16:00.709 --> 01:16:02.077
you two-faced dog.

878
01:16:02.077 --> 01:16:05.147
I can get you one of them, actually.
Very good guard dogs.

879
01:16:05.147 --> 01:16:07.416
They can watch the back and 
the front door at the same time.

880
01:16:07.416 --> 01:16:08.484
I can get you anything you want.

881
01:16:08.484 --> 01:16:11.253
What are the chances of getting
a Babylon candle?

882
01:16:11.253 --> 01:16:12.788
That one's slim.

883
01:16:12.788 --> 01:16:17.359
Although, I did know a girl once,
if you know what I mean ...

884
01:16:17.359 --> 01:16:18.561
I'm a ladies' man.

885
01:16:18.561 --> 01:16:20.062
... who had a sister ...

886
01:16:20.062 --> 01:16:21.230
I think it was a woman.

887
01:16:21.230 --> 01:16:25.067
Oh, she was terrible. Face like 
a bag of frogs. Ugly frogs.

888
01:16:25.067 --> 01:16:26.307
Enough.

889
01:16:43.552 --> 01:16:46.328
Hold on tight!
The Captain's at the helm!

890
01:17:38.807 --> 01:17:41.644
So there's the road 
you'll need for Wall.

891
01:17:41.644 --> 01:17:45.247
Good luck on your journey home,
Yvaine, wherever that may be.

892
01:17:45.247 --> 01:17:48.250
And good luck to you, Tristan,
with your Victoria.

893
01:17:48.984 --> 01:17:50.919
Oh, how can we ever thank you 
enough for your kindness?

894
01:17:50.919 --> 01:17:54.623
Don't mention it.
No, seriously, don't mention it.

895
01:17:54.623 --> 01:17:58.696
Reputations, you know. A lifetime 
to build, seconds to destroy.

896
01:18:01.063 --> 01:18:04.043
Oh, and Tristan, just remember ...

897
01:18:07.169 --> 01:18:08.978
Just think about it.

898
01:18:12.741 --> 01:18:17.155
Well, give my regards to England.
It's been a pleasure to meet you both.

899
01:18:20.582 --> 01:18:23.892
Mind you don't wear that 
wench out, Captain Tristan!

900
01:18:28.557 --> 01:18:30.502
What did he say to you?

901
01:18:31.393 --> 01:18:32.728
What did he say when?

902
01:18:32.728 --> 01:18:35.265
Just then. 
When he whispered to you.

903
01:18:36.732 --> 01:18:39.234
No ... No, he ... He was just saying
we should use the lightning ...

904
01:18:39.234 --> 01:18:41.570
to get you a Babylon candle.

905
01:18:41.570 --> 01:18:43.549
Barter for it, you know.

906
01:18:44.707 --> 01:18:48.313
For the last time, 
where is the girl?

907
01:18:49.645 --> 01:18:52.455
The girl with the stone!
Where did she go?

908
01:18:53.515 --> 01:18:55.751
- Are you mocking me?
- No ...

909
01:18:55.751 --> 01:18:59.289
'Cause if you are, believe me,
you have seconds to live!

910
01:19:04.226 --> 01:19:05.705
What a freak.

911
01:19:05.994 --> 01:19:08.565
Clean this thoroughly.

912
01:19:16.739 --> 01:19:18.081
Bollocks.

913
01:19:20.309 --> 01:19:23.112
If you have quite finished
squandering your magic ...

914
01:19:23.112 --> 01:19:25.714
on your rather
counterproductive beauty routine,

915
01:19:25.714 --> 01:19:28.083
you might like to know that
the star has returned.

916
01:19:28.083 --> 01:19:29.551
She's back on land.

917
01:19:29.551 --> 01:19:33.021
I know, damn it.
I couldn't reach the lake in time.

918
01:19:33.021 --> 01:19:37.493
No matter. We have found her. She is 
on the road to the village of Wall.

919
01:19:37.493 --> 01:19:40.129
If you take the shortcut
across the marshes,

920
01:19:40.129 --> 01:19:42.040
you should arrive in time
to intercept her.

921
01:19:50.973 --> 01:19:52.247
Yvaine.

922
01:19:56.044 --> 01:19:58.247
- Are you trying to break my leg again?
- I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

923
01:19:58.247 --> 01:20:01.283
I just ... I can't risk people 
seeing you. I don't trust anyone.

924
01:20:01.283 --> 01:20:03.051
But at this rate, 
if we keep stopping ...

925
01:20:03.051 --> 01:20:08.057
Yvaine. We're making good time.
Just leave it a minute.

926
01:20:14.997 --> 01:20:16.771
Aren't you tempted?

927
01:20:19.835 --> 01:20:21.609
Tempted? By what?

928
01:20:23.372 --> 01:20:24.816
Immortality.

929
01:20:25.808 --> 01:20:27.719
Let's say it wasn't my heart.

930
01:20:28.243 --> 01:20:31.588
Not me. Just a star 
you didn't know.

931
01:20:33.248 --> 01:20:35.990
You seriously think 
I could kill anyone?

932
01:20:40.055 --> 01:20:44.469
I mean, even if I could ...
Everlasting life?

933
01:20:46.562 --> 01:20:49.167
I imagine it would 
be kind of lonely.

934
01:20:51.366 --> 01:20:52.503
Well,

935
01:20:54.269 --> 01:20:57.079
maybe if you had someone
to share it with.

936
01:20:58.207 --> 01:20:59.845
Someone you love.

937
01:21:00.209 --> 01:21:02.655
Maybe then it might be different.

938
01:21:05.814 --> 01:21:07.987
Come on, I think we're safe.

939
01:21:30.072 --> 01:21:33.815
Now remember, Captain Shakespeare
has a fearsome reputation.

940
01:23:02.064 --> 01:23:04.766
- What the hell is this?
- What are you doing here?

941
01:23:04.766 --> 01:23:06.905
My name is Prince Septimus.

942
01:23:08.103 --> 01:23:10.913
And you're going to tell me
where I can find the girl.

943
01:23:18.180 --> 01:23:23.629
All right, twinkletoes. I'm gonna 
count to three. One, two ...

944
01:23:39.935 --> 01:23:44.706
You know, you sort of glitter sometimes.
I just noticed it. Is it normal?

945
01:23:44.706 --> 01:23:47.909
Let's see if you can work it out
for yourself.

946
01:23:47.909 --> 01:23:49.752
What do stars do?

947
01:23:51.279 --> 01:23:52.951
Attract trouble?

948
01:23:55.017 --> 01:23:57.419
That's ... All right, I'm sorry.
Wait, I'm sorry.

949
01:23:57.419 --> 01:24:00.155
All right, let me ... Let me ...
Do I get another guess?

950
01:24:00.155 --> 01:24:04.228
Is it ... Do they know exactly how
to annoy a boy called Tristan Thorn?

951
01:24:08.463 --> 01:24:11.833
- How long will that take?
- Maybe two days.

952
01:24:11.833 --> 01:24:15.610
But we don't have two days.
Victoria's birthday is tomorrow.

953
01:24:16.338 --> 01:24:19.478
Yes, it is. Well remembered.

954
01:24:25.947 --> 01:24:28.717
- Nice cup of Earl Grey.
- Get out, everybody. Get out.

955
01:24:28.717 --> 01:24:30.719
Did he hurt you, Captain?

956
01:24:32.387 --> 01:24:35.766
Did you tell him where your 
nephew and the girl went?

957
01:24:36.792 --> 01:24:38.669
So what's the problem?

958
01:24:39.895 --> 01:24:41.635
It's my reputation.

959
01:24:43.098 --> 01:24:45.467
No. No, no. Don't be silly. 
Nonsense.

960
01:24:45.467 --> 01:24:47.344
It's all right, Captain.

961
01:24:48.036 --> 01:24:50.539
We always knew you were a whoopsie.

962
01:24:53.809 --> 01:24:57.552
- You'll always be our captain, Captain.
- Aye, aye, Captain.

963
01:25:02.984 --> 01:25:04.052
Captain.

964
01:25:04.052 --> 01:25:06.088
Yeah. Let's go, let's go.

965
01:25:13.228 --> 01:25:15.197
Prince Septimus,
your men, they're dead.

966
01:25:15.197 --> 01:25:16.607
Oh, really?

967
01:25:18.467 --> 01:25:19.673
Go on!

968
01:25:32.747 --> 01:25:35.717
I met that woman.
She's friends with the Captain.

969
01:25:35.717 --> 01:25:37.519
He said she trades at the market
near Wall.

970
01:25:37.519 --> 01:25:39.621
- We could hitch a lift.
- Really? She's a friend of the Captain's?

971
01:25:39.621 --> 01:25:40.895
- You sure?
- Yes, yes.

972
01:25:41.723 --> 01:25:45.594
Wait! Sorry!
My name is Tristan Thorn. This ...

973
01:25:45.594 --> 01:25:47.266
That's my flower.

974
01:25:47.963 --> 01:25:51.133
Eighteen years
I've been looking for that.

975
01:25:51.133 --> 01:25:52.805
Give it to me now!

976
01:25:53.502 --> 01:25:56.671
How dare you?
That was a gift from his mother!

977
01:25:56.671 --> 01:25:57.672
Oh.

978
01:25:58.206 --> 01:26:00.117
Perhaps I was mistaken.

979
01:26:01.376 --> 01:26:02.946
It's all right.

980
01:26:03.445 --> 01:26:05.647
It's obviously very valuable 
to you, so you can have it

981
01:26:05.647 --> 01:26:07.282
in exchange for what I need.

982
01:26:07.282 --> 01:26:09.451
- A Babylon candle?
- And safe passage to the wall.

983
01:26:09.451 --> 01:26:11.386
A Babylon candle?

984
01:26:11.386 --> 01:26:15.423
Oh, no, no, no.
I don't deal in black magic.

985
01:26:15.423 --> 01:26:16.663
Really?

986
01:26:17.259 --> 01:26:19.828
Well, can you give us a lift then?
To the wall?

987
01:26:19.828 --> 01:26:22.899
Well, why didn't you say so
in the first place?

988
01:26:24.900 --> 01:26:27.710
For that flower, 
I can offer you passage.

989
01:26:27.936 --> 01:26:30.205
- Food and lodging on the way?
- Safe passage.

990
01:26:30.205 --> 01:26:32.641
I swear that you will 
arrive at the wall ...

991
01:26:32.641 --> 01:26:35.747
in the exact same condition
that you're in now.

992
01:26:41.316 --> 01:26:44.019
Do you have any idea what
manner of thing it was that you had?

993
01:26:44.019 --> 01:26:46.055
Some kind of lucky charm?

994
01:26:46.488 --> 01:26:49.764
A very lucky charm indeed. 
Protection.

995
01:26:50.559 --> 01:26:55.371
In fact, the exact same thing that
would've prevented me from doing this.

996
01:26:59.734 --> 01:27:02.537
- Oh, my God! What did you do?
- Much better.

997
01:27:02.537 --> 01:27:06.382
You shall not see the star, 
touch it, smell or hear it.

998
01:27:06.708 --> 01:27:10.280
Though I'll keep me word.
You shall not be harmed.

999
01:27:19.187 --> 01:27:22.891
There.
Food and lodging just as I promised.

1000
01:27:22.891 --> 01:27:27.066
Would I be correct in thinking that
you can neither see nor hear me?

1001
01:27:27.462 --> 01:27:30.265
Then I'd like to tell you
that you smell of pee.

1002
01:27:30.265 --> 01:27:32.133
You look like the 
wrong end of a dog.

1003
01:27:32.133 --> 01:27:34.703
And I swear, if I don't get 
my Tristan back as he was,

1004
01:27:34.703 --> 01:27:36.910
I'll be your personal poltergeist!

1005
01:27:56.091 --> 01:27:57.331
Tristan?

1006
01:27:58.860 --> 01:28:01.704
If you can understand me,
look at me now.

1007
01:28:30.191 --> 01:28:33.228
You know when I said
I knew little about love?

1008
01:28:34.929 --> 01:28:36.874
Well, that wasn't true.

1009
01:28:39.367 --> 01:28:41.346
I know a lot about love.

1010
01:28:42.070 --> 01:28:45.540
I've seen it. I've seen 
centuries and centuries of it.

1011
01:28:47.309 --> 01:28:51.313
And it was the only thing that made
watching your world bearable.

1012
01:28:52.480 --> 01:28:57.986
All those wars. 
Pain and lies. Hate.

1013
01:29:00.088 --> 01:29:03.433
Made me want to turn away
and never look down again.

1014
01:29:05.527 --> 01:29:09.372
But to see the way 
that mankind loves.

1015
01:29:10.532 --> 01:29:13.368
I mean, you could search the 
furthest reaches of the universe ...

1016
01:29:13.368 --> 01:29:16.246
and never find 
anything more beautiful.

1017
01:29:19.107 --> 01:29:24.556
So, yes, I know that
love is unconditional.

1018
01:29:26.147 --> 01:29:29.992
But I also know it 
can be unpredictable,

1019
01:29:30.919 --> 01:29:36.596
unexpected, uncontrollable, 
unbearable ...

1020
01:29:38.526 --> 01:29:42.667
and, well, strangely easy 
to mistake for loathing.

1021
01:29:43.398 --> 01:29:44.399
And ...

1022
01:29:46.735 --> 01:29:51.183
what I'm trying 
to say, Tristan, is ...

1023
01:29:52.807 --> 01:29:54.581
I think I love you.

1024
01:29:55.643 --> 01:30:01.388
My heart, it feels like
my chest can barely contain it.

1025
01:30:03.184 --> 01:30:08.656
Like it doesn't belong to me 
anymore. It belongs to you.

1026
01:30:10.158 --> 01:30:14.436
And if you wanted it,
I'd wish for nothing in exchange.

1027
01:30:15.697 --> 01:30:20.703
No gifts, no goods,
no demonstrations of devotion.

1028
01:30:23.705 --> 01:30:26.344
Nothing but knowing you love me, too.

1029
01:30:28.243 --> 01:30:29.813
Just your heart ...

1030
01:30:31.846 --> 01:30:33.586
in exchange for mine.

1031
01:30:43.024 --> 01:30:45.265
The wall is one mile that way.

1032
01:30:46.027 --> 01:30:49.831
There. Though the walk might take
you a little longer than normal.

1033
01:30:49.831 --> 01:30:53.972
Transformation tends to leave the 
brain a bit scrambled for a while.

1034
01:30:57.405 --> 01:30:58.781
You ...

1035
01:30:58.973 --> 01:31:00.008
Oh!

1036
01:31:01.209 --> 01:31:03.621
I warned you. Save your strength.

1037
01:31:04.546 --> 01:31:06.855
I've been so worried about you.

1038
01:31:07.482 --> 01:31:08.756
Victoria.

1039
01:31:09.751 --> 01:31:11.855
I think I preferred "Mother."

1040
01:31:13.721 --> 01:31:18.328
Come on. There's an inn over there.
Victoria's birthday's not till tomorrow.

1041
01:31:19.327 --> 01:31:20.895
I think you need a bath
and a good night's sleep ...

1042
01:31:20.895 --> 01:31:23.264
before you present me to her.

1043
01:31:23.264 --> 01:31:24.936
Come on. Come.

1044
01:31:27.268 --> 01:31:30.214
- Come on, Tristan. It's not that far.
- Okay.

1045
01:31:51.693 --> 01:31:53.495
- Excuse me?
- Oh!

1046
01:31:53.495 --> 01:31:56.771
- I think you're in my bath.
- Well, close your eyes!

1047
01:31:58.266 --> 01:32:02.441
I'm not ... Honestly, I'm not 
looking. Here, I'll turn away.

1048
01:32:07.375 --> 01:32:09.787
All right, you can open them now.

1049
01:32:17.485 --> 01:32:20.659
Did you really mean
what you said in the caravan?

1050
01:32:22.657 --> 01:32:24.025
What I ...

1051
01:32:24.025 --> 01:32:27.096
But ... But you were a mouse!

1052
01:32:27.495 --> 01:32:30.765
You were a mouse! 
You wanted cheese! You didn't ...

1053
01:32:30.765 --> 01:32:32.700
I asked you to give me a sign.

1054
01:32:32.700 --> 01:32:36.909
And risk you being too embarrassed
to keep saying such lovely things?

1055
01:32:42.210 --> 01:32:45.816
You want to know what the Captain
really whispered to me that day?

1056
01:32:48.116 --> 01:32:52.758
He told me that my true love
was right in front of my eyes.

1057
01:32:54.923 --> 01:32:56.595
And he was right.

1058
01:33:11.339 --> 01:33:14.342
Hey, guys! Guys, come on! Come on,
you got to come up and see this!

1059
01:33:14.342 --> 01:33:15.821
No, thank you.

1060
01:33:16.511 --> 01:33:18.615
- Pervert!
- Suit yourself.

1061
01:33:20.548 --> 01:33:23.995
- Ironic, isn't it?
- Yeah. What is?

1062
01:33:24.586 --> 01:33:26.754
Septimus will be 
here by the morning,

1063
01:33:26.754 --> 01:33:30.625
find the girl, get the stone
and be king forever.

1064
01:33:30.625 --> 01:33:32.001
So unjust.

1065
01:33:32.560 --> 01:33:34.903
Still, at least we will be free.

1066
01:33:37.632 --> 01:33:41.069
You're very close.
She's in the market town.

1067
01:33:41.069 --> 01:33:43.471
One mile from the gap in the wall.

1068
01:33:43.471 --> 01:33:45.640
You speak as if this is good news.

1069
01:33:45.640 --> 01:33:49.677
Do I need to remind you that Wall
is not part of our universe?

1070
01:33:49.677 --> 01:33:52.347
If she crosses the threshold
into the human realm,

1071
01:33:52.347 --> 01:33:56.420
our star becomes nothing more than 
a pitted lump of metallic rock.

1072
01:33:56.884 --> 01:33:59.330
Then I suggest you hurry up!

1073
01:34:30.451 --> 01:34:31.657
What?

1074
01:34:32.620 --> 01:34:33.888
Oh, what do you want?

1075
01:34:33.888 --> 01:34:37.125
Sorry. Can I have a 
piece of paper and a pen?

1076
01:34:37.125 --> 01:34:40.094
Ask me again
at a more reasonable hour.

1077
01:34:40.094 --> 01:34:41.295
No, I can't. I have to go.

1078
01:34:41.295 --> 01:34:43.631
Look, if my friend wakes 
up before I get back,

1079
01:34:43.631 --> 01:34:46.134
please, can you give her a message?
- Go on.

1080
01:35:22.704 --> 01:35:26.379
You know, that's the first time
I've ever slept at night.

1081
01:35:27.675 --> 01:35:29.415
I can't believe it.

1082
01:35:33.748 --> 01:35:34.783
Tristan?

1083
01:35:44.025 --> 01:35:45.893
Have you seen my friend?

1084
01:35:45.893 --> 01:35:47.838
He left absurdly early.

1085
01:35:48.996 --> 01:35:50.331
He left?

1086
01:35:50.331 --> 01:35:53.668
He told me to tell you
he's gone to see Victoria ...

1087
01:35:53.668 --> 01:35:56.671
because he's sorry,
but he's found his true love ...

1088
01:35:56.671 --> 01:35:59.845
and he wants to spend
the rest of his life with her.

1089
01:36:00.875 --> 01:36:02.012
What?

1090
01:36:02.977 --> 01:36:05.480
- Are you sure?
- I'm positive.

1091
01:36:34.876 --> 01:36:37.788
Hey! Hey, wait! Wait!

1092
01:37:21.355 --> 01:37:22.890
Happy birthday.

1093
01:37:23.825 --> 01:37:25.167
Tristan.

1094
01:37:27.094 --> 01:37:28.903
What happened to you?

1095
01:37:30.264 --> 01:37:31.936
I found the star.

1096
01:37:34.035 --> 01:37:36.037
I can't believe you did it.

1097
01:37:36.037 --> 01:37:38.540
Where's my star? Can I see it?

1098
01:37:39.640 --> 01:37:41.710
- Is it beautiful?
- Yes.

1099
01:37:43.911 --> 01:37:45.913
Well, it's awfully small.

1100
01:37:46.848 --> 01:37:49.984
Well, that's just a little piece.
A token for your birthday.

1101
01:37:49.984 --> 01:37:52.828
Well ... Well, 
then forget about the star.

1102
01:37:54.088 --> 01:37:56.329
It's not the star that I want.

1103
01:37:58.726 --> 01:38:00.535
You know what I want.

1104
01:38:02.697 --> 01:38:04.574
Yeah. I do.

1105
01:38:09.804 --> 01:38:13.774
You want to grow up
and get over yourself.

1106
01:38:18.546 --> 01:38:20.218
- Humphrey.
- Thorn.

1107
01:38:20.715 --> 01:38:22.854
You must have a death wish.

1108
01:38:30.825 --> 01:38:33.669
Humphrey. Humphrey, it's all right.

1109
01:38:34.262 --> 01:38:36.030
She's all yours.

1110
01:38:36.030 --> 01:38:38.699
You really are a perfect couple.
The best of luck to you both.

1111
01:38:38.699 --> 01:38:43.375
Well, why would I want this?
It's just a measly handful of stardust!

1112
01:38:48.876 --> 01:38:50.047
Yvaine?

1113
01:38:52.146 --> 01:38:54.125
She can't cross the wall.

1114
01:39:02.823 --> 01:39:03.892
Go on!

1115
01:40:11.359 --> 01:40:14.395
Stop! Stop! if you go 
through there, you'll die!

1116
01:40:14.395 --> 01:40:15.498
What?

1117
01:40:17.631 --> 01:40:20.873
If you set foot on human soil,
you'll turn to rock.

1118
01:40:21.402 --> 01:40:24.940
Wretched slattern!
Where have you taken me?

1119
01:40:34.248 --> 01:40:36.851
Planning to enter Wall, were you?

1120
01:40:36.851 --> 01:40:41.299
If death is what you wish, my dear,
I'd be more than happy to assist you.

1121
01:40:41.589 --> 01:40:43.534
Are you talking to me?

1122
01:40:44.225 --> 01:40:45.897
You. Small world.

1123
01:40:46.861 --> 01:40:50.240
Anyhow, no, I wasn't.
I was talking to the star.

1124
01:40:50.698 --> 01:40:52.040
What star?

1125
01:40:53.534 --> 01:40:57.277
My slave girl's no star.
Any fool can see that.

1126
01:40:57.772 --> 01:41:00.508
If she was, I'd have had the 
heart out of her chest ...

1127
01:41:00.508 --> 01:41:03.079
a long time ago, trust me.

1128
01:41:03.444 --> 01:41:06.891
Trust you? Not a mistake
I'd be likely to make again.

1129
01:41:07.515 --> 01:41:10.962
What's it to be, Ditchwater Sal?
Heads or tails?

1130
01:41:40.948 --> 01:41:45.119
- Oh, God. Time to go.
- She's not going anywhere.

1131
01:41:45.119 --> 01:41:47.088
I think you'll find she is.

1132
01:41:47.088 --> 01:41:49.500
It's all right. You can come, too.

1133
01:41:52.359 --> 01:41:56.466
You can ride in the carriage or
be dragged behind it. Your choice.

1134
01:42:16.317 --> 01:42:17.718
What happened?

1135
01:42:17.718 --> 01:42:20.154
Be my guest. I quit.

1136
01:42:20.154 --> 01:42:23.257
Eighty years I've stopped 
you people going out.

1137
01:42:23.257 --> 01:42:25.226
What I should have 
been worrying about ...

1138
01:42:25.226 --> 01:42:26.827
was those people
from the other side coming in.

1139
01:42:26.827 --> 01:42:28.596
All right,
just tell me what happened!

1140
01:42:28.596 --> 01:42:30.507
What happened? Oh, where to start?

1141
01:42:31.832 --> 01:42:32.833
No!

1142
01:42:46.814 --> 01:42:48.054
Come on!

1143
01:43:17.912 --> 01:43:19.083
Go on!

1144
01:43:49.677 --> 01:43:50.951
The star!

1145
01:43:51.512 --> 01:43:52.580
And who else?

1146
01:43:52.580 --> 01:43:55.816
A slave for us. It will be nice to
have someone to help mop up ...

1147
01:43:55.816 --> 01:43:58.118
when we've finished 
with our little guest.

1148
01:43:58.118 --> 01:43:59.620
Oh, good work, sister.

1149
01:43:59.620 --> 01:44:02.566
And just in time, 
I see! You look awful!

1150
01:45:01.548 --> 01:45:02.992
Who are you?

1151
01:45:04.251 --> 01:45:06.594
What business do you have here?

1152
01:45:07.988 --> 01:45:09.296
Septimus?

1153
01:45:09.790 --> 01:45:11.929
I knew your brother, Primus.

1154
01:45:13.227 --> 01:45:15.863
Unless you wish to meet him
in the afterlife,

1155
01:45:15.863 --> 01:45:18.632
I suggest you answer my question.
What are you doing here?

1156
01:45:18.632 --> 01:45:20.975
I might ask you the same thing.

1157
01:45:37.685 --> 01:45:39.596
There are four of them.

1158
01:45:42.523 --> 01:45:45.359
Do as I say and 
we may stand a chance.

1159
01:45:45.359 --> 01:45:47.961
Wait.
How do I know you can be trusted?

1160
01:45:47.961 --> 01:45:50.664
You don't. Why? 
Do you have a choice?

1161
01:45:50.664 --> 01:45:52.700
- No.
- Well, then, let's go.

1162
01:46:00.040 --> 01:46:03.680
Get off me! Get off!

1163
01:46:07.881 --> 01:46:10.184
- Una?
- Septimus.

1164
01:46:10.184 --> 01:46:11.685
- Una!
- Sister!

1165
01:46:30.404 --> 01:46:32.315
Tristan! No! Tristan!

1166
01:46:33.507 --> 01:46:36.249
I'm your ... I'm your ... 
I'm your mother.

1167
01:46:37.444 --> 01:46:39.048
I'm your mother.

1168
01:47:15.716 --> 01:47:18.355
- Rather well.
- Good riddance to you. Yes.

1169
01:47:43.911 --> 01:47:44.912
Oh!

1170
01:47:57.925 --> 01:48:00.166
Let's put out those flames.

1171
01:48:00.561 --> 01:48:01.903
Shall we?

1172
01:48:52.579 --> 01:48:54.490
So, what happens now?

1173
01:48:55.249 --> 01:48:57.751
We have to learn 
to live with each other.

1174
01:48:57.751 --> 01:48:58.991
Forever?

1175
01:49:04.458 --> 01:49:06.267
Let's get on with it.

1176
01:49:10.163 --> 01:49:12.199
Be the man that I know you are.

1177
01:49:29.950 --> 01:49:31.351
- Get outside.
- Okay.

1178
01:49:31.351 --> 01:49:32.386
Go.

1179
01:49:42.529 --> 01:49:44.440
- Tristan!
- Get him.

1180
01:50:20.067 --> 01:50:21.375
Get off!

1181
01:50:25.472 --> 01:50:27.246
Get out!

1182
01:51:04.378 --> 01:51:08.883
So, what's it to be, Prince Charming?
Frog or tadpole?

1183
01:52:06.907 --> 01:52:08.181
Septimus?

1184
01:53:30.257 --> 01:53:31.463
Tristan!

1185
01:54:15.235 --> 01:54:16.577
Youth.

1186
01:54:17.470 --> 01:54:18.812
Beauty.

1187
01:54:20.574 --> 01:54:23.054
It all seems meaningless now.

1188
01:54:23.710 --> 01:54:25.712
My sisters are dead!

1189
01:54:27.314 --> 01:54:31.023
Everything I cared about gone.

1190
01:54:37.157 --> 01:54:38.294
Go.

1191
01:54:43.730 --> 01:54:45.072
Go!

1192
01:55:06.052 --> 01:55:07.053
No!

1193
01:56:04.144 --> 01:56:06.123
I owe you thanks, boy.

1194
01:56:06.513 --> 01:56:09.653
What use was her heart to me
when it was broken?

1195
01:56:10.050 --> 01:56:14.999
And you got rid of my sisters and 
now I can have it all for myself.

1196
01:56:20.827 --> 01:56:23.898
- Hold me tight and close your eyes.
- What? Why?

1197
01:56:24.631 --> 01:56:26.303
What do stars do?

1198
01:56:33.073 --> 01:56:34.279
Shine.

1199
01:57:10.477 --> 01:57:12.183
- Bravo!
- Yeah!

1200
01:57:12.545 --> 01:57:13.887
Very good.

1201
01:57:15.615 --> 01:57:17.250
Why didn't you do that earlier?

1202
01:57:17.250 --> 01:57:21.926
I couldn't have done that without you.
No star can shine with a broken heart.

1203
01:57:23.289 --> 01:57:25.200
I thought I'd lost you.

1204
01:57:26.426 --> 01:57:28.269
But you came back.

1205
01:57:28.628 --> 01:57:30.971
Of course I did. I love you.

1206
01:57:36.503 --> 01:57:37.777
Come on.

1207
01:57:57.824 --> 01:57:59.166
Tristan.

1208
01:58:01.861 --> 01:58:05.399
The last surviving male heir
of the Stormhold bloodline.

1209
01:58:08.134 --> 01:58:09.806
It's you, Tristan.

1210
01:58:13.640 --> 01:58:16.677
But if he's the new 
King of Stormhold, then ...

1211
01:59:03.690 --> 01:59:08.229
I crown thee 
King Tristan of Stormhold.

1212
01:59:25.578 --> 01:59:27.022
Yeah!

1213
01:59:42.896 --> 01:59:44.568
My gift to you.

1214
01:59:46.332 --> 01:59:47.776
To you both.

1215
01:59:48.034 --> 01:59:49.410
Thank you.

1216
02:00:04.317 --> 02:00:06.922
They ruled for 80 years.

1217
02:00:08.121 --> 02:00:11.101
But no man can live forever,

1218
02:00:11.958 --> 02:00:15.405
except he who possesses
the heart of a star.

1219
02:00:16.262 --> 02:00:20.710
And Yvaine had given hers
to Tristan completely.

1220
02:00:22.235 --> 02:00:25.580
When their children and 
grandchildren were grown,

1221
02:00:26.573 --> 02:00:29.849
it was time to 
light the Babylon candle.

1222
02:00:35.882 --> 02:00:40.353
And they still live 
happily ever after.
